Gary Whitehead <gary@unidec.co.uk> wrote:
}I have been asked the following question:
}"How good is the clock on a DEC Alpha? Our customer wants
}to know if it is better than 300 seconds / year?"

    That's about 1 part in 100,000
    and that's certainly "in the ballpark"
    Checking a machine here, I see it has drifted .045 sec
    in 4.5 hours (extrapolates to about 100 seconds/year).

    If you want "arbitrarily" accurate (within small fractions
    of a second of the actual time) use ntp/xntpd (see their
    man pages).  Most of our clients just set their time
    every 12 hours with ntp -- critical servers can use
    xntpd to constantly adjust the clock to stay *very*
    accurate AND to insure no jumps in the time space.

John
