content-type: TEXT/PLAIN; charset=ISO-8859-1
organization: ICL, Finland
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


The DECpc ST used original Intel Xpress architecture (motherboard
& CPU-daughtercard).

There is no Alpha CPU-card for the DECpc ST -series, but the first 
Alpha/NT box, the DECpc AXP 150 was based on the DECpc ST-design/
enclosure.

The 433ST can handle a 486DX4 (~100Mhz), or a 83MHz Pentium OverDrive,
however, the ST's had two variants of CPU-daughtercards; those with 
socketed CPUs, and others without.

If the CPU-card is one with a soldered CPU, you'll have a hard time
removing it, and replacing it with a socket, I'm afraid.

If the CPU is socketed, then you can just remove it and put in a "new"
66MHz 486DX2 or 100MHz 486DX4 or a 83MHz Pentium OverDrive (for a 486
socket, not an OverDrive socket).

If you jumper the CPU-card for 25MHz, then you can also use, e.g., 
a 63MHz Pentium OverDrive.

There was also a 60MHz Pentium (not OverDrive) CPU-card for the ST.

If you can & do upgrade the CPU, get the latest BIOS and ECU (EISA
Configuration Utilities) from http://www.windows.digital.com


regards,
