I am the person responsible for the 1.5GFlop/sec performance result on
the chilled Alpha system runnings Windows NT.  This was certainly a fun
technology demonstration that grew out out of some work I did for the
SuperComputing 96 show.  That involved taking an AlphaStation 500/500
running Unix, and tweaking the clock speed up just a bit; I was able to
achieve a sustainable performance of 1.02 GFlop/sec on a simple,
straightforward Fortran 90 program I wrote to evaluate polynomials.

The standard 500 MHz AlphaStation 500/500 runs the identical code at
998 MFlop/sec.  And I was not counting any of the integer operations
that were performed in this.

Best part of this is that I have carried this desktop GigaFlop system
around under my arm!

