newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



In article <32ED0942.5468@post.its.mcw.edu>, Ron Kneusel <rkneusel@post.its.mcw.edu> writes:
>

My low opinion of the 11/730 is largely a result of having had
one of our main support system when I worked at the Customer
Support Center supporting ULTRIX on it.  At least until we
got a MicroVAX II, which felt like a quantum leap in performance.

>Is it worth taking one of these machines? (no cost..)

Not unless you need a largish boat anchor or a large closet heater.

>
>The unit has the following installed (what these are I don't know..)
>
>H7202B   - power supply
>RB730-A  - ???

Integrated disk controller.  In typical configurations is supported
the R80, a 14" platter ~120 MB disk and an RL02, which was a
10 MB removable cartridge drive (also 14" platter).  I think the
R80 (also RB80) was the same HDA we used in the RM80 and RA80,
but I'm not sure.  You'd be hard pressed to find working RB80
drives, which you'd need for any remotely reasonable operating
system.  I think OpenVMS may still support it, if only because
they probably have to support everything.  ULTRIX gave up on it
years ago, though the code may still be there.

There was an option to have a UNIBUS on the system which slightly
increases the options for storage.

>DMF32-A  - ???

A combination communcations controller; 8 serial lines, some with
modem control, some without, a parallel interface and a synchronous
interface.

>KC730    - ???

The CPU, most of it anyway.  I think all the microcode that made
the underlying system a VAX is loaded off the TU58s.  The system
supports a maximum of 5 MB of memory.

>FP730    - ???

A floating point accelerator, which sped up some of the integer
instructions and the floating point instructions.  I use the
term "sped up" loosely.
>
>Besides being a large-ish boat anchor, what else can I do with this
>machine?

Put it in a closet and run VAX H floating point (*) very slowly?  It
prefers to have the closet ventilated.  If you find disks, they
are less tolerant of the heat than the system as I recall.  

It really is a very slow system being about 1/3rd the speed of a
VAX 11/780 (The SPEC 1989 and 1992 benchmark machine).  And this
just for pure CPU bound code that doesn't require paging.  Still,
being able to run VAX H Floating point was one few advantages.
For people needing that and not minding that it might take a
long time to run anything useful, it was the least expensive
VAX for a long time.

(*) A 128 bit floating format.

>How hard would it be to add a hard drive?

If you can find a working RB80 or can survive with an RL02
probably not very hard.  Otherwise, very hard, I'd think.

>Any pointers to info on these machines?

A VAX Hardware Handbook circa 1986.

--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




