Per Burstr�m wrote:
> 
> From http://www.sscs.org/isscc/1997/ap/FA10.htm
> 
> " This quad-issue, 550MHz microprocessor includes hardware support for
> MPEG-II encode and decode through addition of 13 instructions to the
> Alpha architecture. The 1.38cm2 die in a 0.35mm four-layer-metal process
> includes 3.5M transistors and is estimated to deliver 15SPECint95 and
> 20SPECfp95. "
> 
> Is this the 21164PC? Can it encode MPEG-II in real-time? Where can I
> read more about it?

As long as you don't specify a resolution, encoded bit rate, or anything
else, I'll assert that today's Alpha processors will encode MPEG-2 in
real-time.  The 21164PC will do video encoding (MPEG-1, MPEG-2, H.261
and H.263) several times faster than today's 21164.  The biggest speedup
comes from the new pixel error (PERR) instruction which gives the sum
of the absolute value of the differences between two sets of eight
pixels.
It has a latency of 2 cycles and is fully pipelined as compared to
the 10 or 12 cycles (I don't remember exactly) required to do the same
thing today.

There have been a couple of articles in EE Times on the 21164PC that
provide additional details on the chip and new instructions.

You can also find a slide presentation on it at

	http://www.digital.com/info/semiconductor/mvi/index.html


You can find the 21264 presentation at

	http://www.digital.com/info/semiconductor/a264up1/index.html

