On Thu, 30 Jan 1997 21:24:13 +1000, Robert Brockway <robert@humbug.org.au> wrote:
>: Linus is said to be running RedHat on his Alpha, there are some guys at
>: Digital developing (are you still, Digtial guys?) stuff, and most code
>: developed for Intel is easily compiled and run on Alpha as well.
>
>A little history:  There were 2 alpha ports.  Linux was doing a 64bit one
>while some dec people were doing a 32bit one.  The 32bit one was dropped,
>and the once we see is the 64bit one.  Afaik Linus is still the primary
>developer of the alpha port.  He certainly did all the hard work.  About
>85% of the kernel code is platform independent anyway.

While Linus did do a lot of the kernel work for Linux/Alpha, there are some
other names that figure prominently in the port and are a bit (probably
unintentionally) disparaged by the above...

Jim Paradis and Jay Estabrook abandoned the 32 bit Alpha port once Linus
decided to work on a 64 bit port and still spend their time porting Linux
to new boards, getting XFree86 up and running (and porting new servers),
getting various SCSI cards working, writing drivers for odd video cards,
etc. David Mosberger did the same type of work as Jim and Jay, but he did
it for free. He also did a ton of work on the Alpha userland and libc, 
including working on glibc and ELF. Richard Henderson merely (!) fixes gcc
bugs, writes highly efficient exception handling for 2.1 kernels, rewrite
the module utils to work with 2.1 and be multiarchitecture, fixes 
linker and assembler bugs (and rewrote /usr/bin/as for the Alpha), and
made large contributions to glibc and ELF.

I'm sure I forgot some key peopl. While the port would not have reached the
stability and maturity without Linus help (and probably more importantly, his
leadership) their are a lot of incredibly bright people working on 
Linux/Alpha.

Erik

