In news:x7zpxuld2g.fsf@dumbcat.codewright.com, Marc Hyman
(marc@dumbcat.codewright.com) wrote --

> Uhhh, Dan, WordStar didn't exactly use mnemonic command characters.
> Mind you, I'm glad they didn't.  Some people prefer mnemonic commands;
> others (I include myself in this group) prefer positional commands.
> We loved WordStar because the ^S ^E ^D ^X made sense whereas ^B ^P
> ^F ^N (emacs version of the same commands) were hard to remember.

Marc, you're not being entirely fair to WordStar -- or maybe you've
forgotten the commands that *are* (supposedly!) mnemonic (note the use of
the present tense): ^O (onscreen formatting), ^K (block operations), ^P
(print enhancements, ^U (undo), ^L (last [most recent] search or replace
operation), ^Q (quicken a cursor movement or scrolling key)....
 
So -- on the left hand, positional commands for positional functions; on
the right hand, mnemonic commands for functions requiring more thought
and involving many options; in the middle of the keyboard (where you have
to reach a bit if you use proper touch-typing technique), destructive
functions, placed there to minimize disasters in the days before undo.
 
There are a few unavoidable departures and inconsistencies, and a few
improvements that can be made within the basic framework, but these
detract little from the whole. I personally find its very elegance a
sort of mnemonic.
 
> Alas, I gave up on WordStar when I found a CPM editor that let me have
> split screen, multiple open files, etc.  The fact that it used emacs
> mnemonics just meant it was a bit harder for me to learn.
 
Yes, WordStar falls down in many other areas. It eventually got a second
document window and other features that people value, but it was too
little, too late. Hardly a day goes by that I don't wonder why, why, why.
 
During the past couple of years I've become familiar with the emacs
movement keys, mostly through Lynx and Elm. They seem ridiculously
awkward, physically -- and I do remember the slight initial awkwardness
of certain WordStar keystrokes -- plus they involve both hands for
simple cursor movement. Alternating hands for `forward' and `back'...
whew! Hard to learn or not (which matters little when you're talking
about a lifetime skill), I *suspect* they would be slower even after
being mastered. Dunno. I now use WordStarized versions of DOS
WordPerfect and Unix JOE.... haven't found anything with features
attractive enough to make me give up WordStar keystrokes. (To get around
the one-file limit, I used to read multiple files into a single scratch
file. Didn't even know about multiple-file editors at the time, though.)
 
Dan Strychalski
dski@cameonet.cameo.com.tw



