John Hendrickx (J.Hendrickx@maw.kun.nl) wrote --

> Gideon <gideon.marcus@medtronic.com> wrote:
>
>>      ....I do think GUIs need to be rethought.  It is the stupidest thing
>> that half the time I type something in, I have to use my mouse to click
>> "enter".  There is a reason we have a key labeled "ENTER" right?
>>
> A troll, or not a troll, that is the question. Actually, a lot of
> GUI-newbies really think they *have* to click something, don't realize
> that hitting ENTER selects the highlighted button.

Yup. Many seem not even to know what menus are for. When I started at my
current job in '94, I found an office full of people who had been using
Word for who knows how long but didn't know how to change margins or add
graphics. They were printing on U.S. letter-size sheets with Word set for
A4, and gluing the company's letterhead onto their printouts for faxing.

When the GUI comes along that treats Ctrl-M as Return/Enter, I'll
consider it.

>>  Have icons maybe for file copy, but a command line just as potent
>> underneath?  (Not just a Windows '95 "Oh yeah--there are shortcut keys...
>> somewhere).
>>
> Let's see. You're perfectly willing to learn a fat manual full of CLI
> commands, but a few important shortcuts exceeds your storage capacity.

About a dozen commands are all any ordinary user needs to remember. This
does not seem excessive for a tool that one can expect to use nearly
every day for the rest of one's life.

> BTW, on a Mac you can get an extension called 'Now Menus'. Just select a
> menu item, press the shortcut key, and presto. The shortcut appears on
> the menu as a reminder.

Sounds like WordStar, 1979 -- except that then you could do everything
without removing your hands from the home position, and every keystroke
you might need was displayed right there on the screen. Any fool could
see that a shell for the o.s. could be designed along the same lines. We
waited and waited... and watched while the industry made keyboard use
harder and harder, with unmnemonic, undisplayed keystrokes scattered
everywhere BUT in the main block, and with unnecessarily confusing
switches and options for often-used utilities (think of the difference
between `dir /ad' and `dir /od' -- oh, those folks are slick; or try to
figure out MODE.COM typematic rate settings).

And to those who say we should discuss only what is, not what could be, I
say _wrong_. To paraphrase the comp.human-factors FAQ, human factors are
the ONLY factors. Systems are designed by human beings, and there is a
reason for every decision those human beings make.

Dan Strychalski
dski@cameonet.cameo.com.tw



