Iain A F Fleming <iainf@bri.st.com> wrote:

> This use of assembly is probably the only shorcoming of this otherwise
> truly brilliant series - the books would be far more accessible to
> modern high-level language programmers if Knuth had chosen Algol (or
> suchlike) to express the algorithms.

He's still planning to use assembler (now MMIX rather than MIX)
in the upcoming new edition of The Art of Computer Programming,
though.  The rationale is that high level languages come and go,
but ultimately they all have to compile down to a machine level,
and the human-readable version of an algorithm can be expressed
as well in English as in a programming language.  I don't know
whether I agree with that argument, but that's what it is.

For the full story, see http://www-cs-faculty.stanford.edu/~knuth/mmix.html

Eric



