In article <Pine.SOL.3.91.970125181353.28564A-100000@student.canberra.edu.au>, "Maftoum / Karl Joseph (ISE)" <u963870@student.canberra.edu.au> writes:
:my vaxstation 3100 has the model number VS542A-BC..what model is this?

  Well, it's an invalid model number :-)

  Digital part numbers use a 2-5-2 scheme, where the prefix and/or the
  suffix and/or a couple of characters in the 5 might be omitted, and
  (when present) where the prefix and the suffix indicate the "class"
  of the part number, and the variant, respectively.

:I suspect it is a 38 but I'm not quite sure...

  Usually more interesting is the processor model number displayed
  when the system powers up.  (The medallions and external stickers
  sometimes don't get changed during a "midnight" upgrade.  :-)

	KA42-AA VAXstation 3100 models 30 and 40
	KA42-BA VAXstation 3100 models 38 and 48
	KA43-AA VAXstation 3100 model 76

  The model 3x differs from the model 4x differs solely in enclosure
  size: the model 4x enclosure series in either pair is physically
  somewhat taller (circa 3" vs 4" tall), and contains more expansion
  (circa 3 vs 5) slots.

  This information is from memory, and may contain errors.

