I used one as a home computer for quite a while.  The tapes
are about 92kb capacity and are good only for boot, and
slow for that.  As I remember, the microcode out-loads
from the tape boot process.

I discarded mine long ago.  My opinion is that it is good
only if you can find a third party disk controller and
scsi disk AND a way to load VMS or unix  software onto 
the scsi disk.

The boot tapes are a **requirement** in either case.

I believe the RB730 is a controller for a RA80 disk.  
Consider it useless other than a way to get a source of
software. Obviously, the tape is not usable for this chore.  

I think the FP730 is floating point.  The CPU is a multi-
board set, but I don't remember how many boards.
DEC stated a maximum memory capacity that was below actual 
capacity.  Add in memory was (is?) available from third
party soruces.  (National, at least)  Some of the used
equipment vendors may be a source.  

Basically the top-to-bottom card layout was RB730/cpu & 
memory/unibus interfaces.  Many interfaces are available
including 9 track tape, etc.

Still, I wouldn't haul it off.



