Someone did create a 'look and blink' prototype for disabled people.
(I hate not being able to remember who or give a cite.)  While it's
useful for people with no arms but full head mobility, it puts a
great strain on the neck and eye muscles, since people aren't used
to using them that precisely.  People who could type never used the
apperatus in preference to typeing because they found it too tiring.
The developers also had to drop the resolution of the screen by (I
think) 16 times linear -- limitations of equipment.

Strangely enough, they found that in the same way that people
'type-ahead' and 'mouse-ahead', they 'look-ahead': when the user
blinked, it had to click where they'd been looking one or two fifths
of a second before-hand.  Should this have been obvious ?  I dunno.

Simon.
