I've found "word processors" as WordPerfect to be, well, basically
useless.  It takes no longer to learn to use nroff/troff, TeX/Latex,
Mint, Lout, etc. in conjunction with Emacs or vi, than it does to read
through the long and annoyng documentation that comes with those
so-called "word processors" like WordPerfect.  Besides, nroff/troff,
TeX/LaTeX, Mint, Lout, Scribe, etc. are much more useful and create
nicer results with less work.

As far as "word processors" go, WordPerfect can be one's worst
nightmare.  WordStar can run rather well in a small amount of RAM on a
floppy-based CP/M or MP/M system, which makes it rather useful for
many things.  Actually, a combination of using WordStar to creats
ASCII text, plus the use of a Scribe-like document formatter, might
mit be a bad idea for small systems -- I seem to recall reading about
a small version of Scribe for CP/M and MP/M systems.

