Ron Kneusel <rkneusel@post.its.mcw.edu> wrote:

>I hope this is the appropriate newsgroup..

>A friend's company is dumping several VAX 11/730-ZA computers.  They do
>not have hard drives but do have one or two 8mm tape drives.  It appears
>that they booted to some built-in code from which the operators would
>choose a program to be run from tape.  The only thing I could find that 
>even remotely resembled "docs" was a page for the techs who had to
>restart the machines.  The steps were:

First the tape are not 8mm they are tu58 dectape.  They were used for
two purposes, system microcode load and system boot.  The other use
was system diagnostic.

Without a hard disk most Vax regardless of the version are not of much
use.  Generally though 730s had either a ra80 or ra81 disk in the top
or bottom third of the rack.  The other dive was usually an RL02 or
RA60 removable pack.  As Vaxen go they were slow and limited in
expansion.

I used one as a single user it was a fair machine and ran a lot of
work through one.

