In article <5ce95k$aqv@sun.rhrk.uni-kl.de>, etteldor@unix-ag.uni-kl.de (Martin Etteldorf) writes:
|>
|>Hi there,
|>	I've just bought a VaxStation II/GPX for a few bucks.
|>It has 13 Megs of Ram (M7606 CPU/ 1x 4MB Board/ 1x M7609 8MB Board),
|>M7555, Dilog MQ696 ESDI Controller, Emulex AAD1965 4xSerial Interface,
|>1x M7169, 2x M7168, 1x M7546, 1x M7504, 1x M9058
|>When I switch on the Machine, the HD's (1x Fujitsu M2246, 2x Micropolis 1325)
|>spin up but nothing else happens. The Monitor remains blank, and even the
|>serial Terminal shows nothing.
|>As I have got no Documentation with this Machine and the only Vax I've ever
|>worked with was a 3100 I don't know what could cause this. I already have 
|>removed all Cards but the CPU and the HD Controllers but even then the 
|>Machine won't boot :-(
|>

When removing cards be sure to either shuffle up the other cards or to
add grant cards to fill the gaps otherwise the machine will experience
odd problems - you need to preserve grant continuity.

From the number of drives you have installed, it sounds like you are using
a BA123 box rather than the smaller BA23. (The BA23 is approximately
160mmx720mmx250mm, the BA123 is bigger). Grant continuity for the BA23 looks 
something like:

	Slot	A	B	C	D
	  1	    +
		    |
	  2	    +
		    |
	  3	    +
		    |
	  4	    +----------------+
				     |
	  5	    +----------------+
		    |
	  6	    +----
			etc.

i.e. the top three slots are where CPU and memory go. Double width cards
can also go in slots 2 & 3 but they must be on the AB side. If you then fill up
the rest of the bus with double width cards they should go in slot 4AB,
slot 4CD slot 5CD, slot 5AB, slot 6AB etc. to preserve grant. Quad width
cards automatically preserve grant and can go anywhere (except in the first
3 slots, unless they are CPU or memory). The BA123 is similar except that I 
think it is the first four slots that are special (but I think there is
some indication on the box that this is the case).

If you remove the M7168 and M7169 cards you won't get any output on the monitor
as they are the graphics cards. In that case you need the terminal set up on
the console.

In your postion I would do more or less what you have done: start with 
CPU and memory and see if you get any output on the terminal when you
power up. (The terminal should be set to 9600 8 bits no parity but you 
may need to check the console switch to verify this). When you power up
you should see something within about a minute (my uVAX III boards take
something up to 20s to sort themselves out before outputting anything). If
you don't, check the 7-segment LED by the baud rate switch. It should count
down in hex as it self-tests. If you don't see anything here, then you have
probably miswired one or both of the cables from the CPU to the console.

Assuming you can now get to the >>> prompt, add a disk controller and try to
boot. (You need a bootable drive for this, obviously). Once that works, add
the M7168,M7169 boards and get your monitor back online (assuming it works).
Then add the rest of the cards.

Hope this helps.

Antonio

