Several years ago, my neighbor told me he bought a new PC, a 486 that
cost, at the time, over $3K.  He kept telling me how powerful it is, and
he absolutely needs it for work.  "Otherwise, I'll get left behind" he
told me.  He sells real estate.  He wanted me to come over and see it,
which I finally did.

When I got there, I found he had this ridiculous program (he called it
"the computer" as though there was nothing else on it) that was a GUI
looking like an office.  If he clicked on a notepad, he got a text
processor that put him through unbelievable contortions just to make a
note.  If he clicked on the clock he got the time, which he could have
done by looking at his watch.  If he clicked on the outbox he could send
e-mail to his daughter, whom he could have called.  At least that was
supposed to happen ... he didn't know he had to plug it into a phone
jack.  Nothing, except possibly the notepad, was the least bit useful in
real-estate.

All this for $3K!!!  I'd like to get my hands on the salesman that
shamed him into buying it.  I'm aware that the PC is capable of a lot
more, but why tell him ... he couldn't use what he had, and what he had
was nothing!
