In article <32ef18bb.2325303@firewall.unidec.co.uk>, gary@unidec.co.uk (Gary Whitehead) writes:

:"How good is the clock on a DEC Alpha? Our customer wants
:to know if it is better than 300 seconds / year?"
:The machine in question has the pci233 Digital Alpha motherboard with
:a 21066 chip in it.

   The specified (Architectural) *WORST* case for the system clock is:

	VAX TOY: .0025% accuracy, approximately 65 seconds a month.
        Alpha BB_WATCH: .005% (50ppm), about 130 seconds per month.

   The *ACTUAL* accuracy may/will be better than this value...

   The TOY (time-of-year) and BB_WATCH (battery-backed watch) clocks
   -- there are various software- and hardware-implemented time-keepers
   available for/on most Alpha platforms -- will keep time whether or
   not power is applied to the system.

   I do not have the specifications for the particular Alpha processor
   specified -- check the hardware documentation -- to see if it has
   higher BB_WATCH accuracy specified.

   External time sources are available for nearly any computer platform.
   There are various solutions around, including WWV receivers, packages
   that use a modem to contact a time-server such as the US NIST folks,
   the "cesium (`atomic') clock PCI option" :-), various PCI or ISA
   widgets, and packages that use GPS signals.

   One can also use network time-servers, and protocols such as NTP or
   DECdtss, depending on the host software -- and NTP and DECdtss have
   the added advantage of slowly "drifting" the clock to the "correct"
   value, when a time-change is required...

   The appropriate solution depends on the operating system, and on the
   specific application requirements.

  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
