Actually, the way I hear it, affinity means you can use VMS WITH
NT systems, in networks etc., smoothly, not that NT can run VMS
apps or vice versa, and the Bristol piece doesn't really fit that.

Duplicating the entire NT system service set on VMS, or the entire
VMS system service set on NT, or both, are probably doable, but 
that isn't the intention, at least not as I understand it presently.

It is not un-doable to imagine either or both of these being done,
and would make for a very interesting sort of situation, but MS is
pretty secretive about what their system services even look like.

Open system...hah! What a lie! It is even more closed than the Mac OS,
where I think the system calls were at least all published. With NT
I believe that is not even done. 

Coming from a VMS background where not only the system calls, but also
a considerable number of internal entry points, have been documented
for years, this is a major change. It makes it real hard on anyone who
might try either of these things.

Right now, VMS in affinity is trying to fix it so that as the engine handling
serving, backup, data integrity and security, etc., you won't know that
it isn't another NT box, except of course for the lack of need to reboot
it N times a day, the ability to actually restore backups after a long
time and get everything, the kind of security that comes from having
been in the wars for a number of years, etc.

The Bristol stuff gives a part of a common API, usable for some apps,
but far from all of it.
glenn



