In article <1997Jan23.174857.1@eisner>, kilgallen@eisner.decus.org (Larry Kilgallen) writes:
>
>Every time you modify a piece of software, you risk introducing
>significant new defects (significant = users will notice).  The
>choice to make a modification should be made only if the goal of
>the change (known) is more important that the effect of defects
>you might introduce (unknown).
>

Absolutely - no change is so small that it can't screw up in a major way.

The classic case of this was reported in the papers and took place at
Pacific Bell several years ago.  They were modifying their billing code so
that people wouldn't get billed on wrong numbers - they would now not bill
for calls of 3 seconds (or something like that) or less duration.  When it
went into production there was an immediate large drop in billing revenue. 
It took them a while to notice it and find the problem - amazingly, this
code was not checking the fields for minutes and hours!  If my memory of
the article in the paper serves, they were able to recalculate charges
correctly for several days prior to the time they finally located the
error, but didn't have data tapes going back to the time of the change, and
lost several million dollars for uncharged phone calls. 

Regards,

David Mathog
