Douglas Borsom wrote:
 
> >We considered moving the LGA code to an SGI R10000 system or
> >alpha (from x86), to take advantage of the 64-bit registers,
> >but I was scared off by the byte-addressing problem and the
> >porting time.  About that time, I decided to go to lattice
> >boltzmann methods instead of LGA, and we will eventually get
> >to compare those on alpha and PPro.

> Isn't the easy way around these problems to go with an  Alpha that
> supports byte/word opperations: the  366/433/500 MHz systems?

The push to go with a RISC system was made in 1994.  At that time,
my boss wanted to spread the cost for an R8000-based SGI Power
Challenge, with the promise that there would be an upgrade to
R10000 "when available".

The better solution was (in the long run) to go to a completely
different algorithm, based on floating point calculations.  That
is why you have seen me solicit comparisons of similarly FP-
and memory-intensive benchmarks on a variety of systems.

If we had stuck with the original method, we might now have
reason to stay with x86: the MMX instruction set.  The basic
LGA algorithm does ORs, ANDs and ADDs in parallel on N bytes,
where N is the byte size of the registers.  Only 6 registers
are required to hold the basic data.

> Specint95/Specfp95 10.7/10.2. 
> Of course if folks insist on comparing Intel's current top-of-the-line
> chip with older Alpha technology, they can come up with a win for the
> PPro.

I have asked to purchase an alpha, but my boss asks me to
justify the purchase with proof that it really will perform
much better on the specific codes I run.  Not a bad call.
In the next few weeks I'll try to clean up the current test code,
stripping off the graphics and making it less OS dependent.



