>>>> other stuff that should look good). But GUI is not as good for managing
>>>> systems where command-line is more productive to those who know it well.
>>> Just why IMHO GUI should be left to the realm of applications where
>>> it can make a difference, and not to the realm of system administration.
   
   A GUI has the advantage that it maps to humans better for providing
   a "close enough" view of whatever the computer is doing at the
   moment under the covers.  This is extremely useful for providing
   the quickest LOOK at all sorts of arcane and arbitrary data which
   smart systems administrators can use to decide whether or not the
   machine needs attention, is running fine, or needs attention NOW!

   However, if that GUI is like the MAC interface where all you really
   get is the GUI, then the computer has been idiot-proofed beyond
   usefulness and is really not suited for those applications where
   knowing the difference between what appears to be going on and
   what IS going on is critical.  

   In other words, if you don't have the ability to burrow under that
   nice convenient GUI and get into a overriding command mode that
   allow you to enter the "Listen here you stupid lump of silicon, 
   YOU are the computer, and I am the human, so stop screwing around
   and tell me EXACTLY what you are up to and do EXACTLY what I tell
   you to do or I will melt you back into sand and dump you in the
   Iraqi desert" mode, then you really would never never want to
   count on that machine for anything more critical than playing games
   doing graphics, or other "toy" functions. 


   However, I personally am looking forward to the days when in 
   addition to the "listen here you piece of crap" CLI, you can
   normally operate a computer with the IOI, or Intuitive Operator
   Interface.  The IOI mode of operation is based on the principle
   that "listen here you stupid computer, if you are so damned smart,
   why don't you just figure out what it is I WANT you to do rather
   than what I TOLD you to do and stop bugging me with those 
   supercilious error messages".    Rumors that this IOI is being
   worked on by a team of programmers consisting solely of mothers
   of 2-year-olds keep surfacing.   

   Voice Input is really dangerous as computers become smarter and
   more powerful.  If you don't have a good IOI by the time computers
   become powerful enough to perform just about any input speech 
   command, computer operators everywhere will be plagued by having
   to constantly retrieve their computers from: the bottom of the
   ocean, the depths of the nearest cesspool, or the deepest fires
   of hell.    


