In article <5c3hi5$euc@news.usf.edu>, jra@scfn.thpl.lib.fl.us (Jay R. Ashworth) writes:
>Tony Scandora 630-252-7541 (scandora@cmt.anl.gov) wrote:
>:                                Look at UNIX.  Most UNIX systems today don't
>: even run a layered product capable of providing batch and print queue services
>: inferior to what was in VMS V1.0, and UNIX security lets almost anyone become
>: root via sendmail bugs and countless programs that let long command lines dance
>: on the stack,
>
>Funny, my _free_ copy of Linux doesn't seem to have all these problems.
>Again, commercial versions of Unix suffer from the same syndrome.

Read the CERT mailings and see just how vulnerable too many systems are.
I'm amazed at the problems in current releases of major vendors' software.

>If they can.  The platform is not the _most_ important poart, no... but
>how many Oracle7 database systems do you see running on DOS boxen?

Bringing up DOS is no different from bringing up RT-11.  A single user,
unprotected system with a toy file system is irrelevant.

>: NT V4.0 and SQL Server V6.5 are not even close to enterprise worthy.  
>
>Glad we agree on this.
>
And it just got worse.  The papers are full of stories of customers who got
burned by NT V4.0's Service Pack 2, some badly, all while Microsoft says there
are no serious problems with it.  Such incompetence and arrogance should hurt
the company, but Microsoft will probably quietly fix the problems before any
UNIX or VMS vendors take advantage of the situation.  V5.0 or V6.0 or V7.0 or
something else will eventually come out some day and will work.  Will the UNIX
and VMS vendors be ready?

>We'll see, I guess.  Until NT runs on anything except x86en and Alphas,
>I doubt it.   And that 2 processor limit...

NT was ported to Power PC and MIPS, but those were killed due to lack of
demand.  Should Sun port NT to SPARC?  The 2 processor limit is for what they
call clusters, which are nothing to take seriously.  The SMP limit is higher;
Digital has announced or leaked rumors of a TurboLaser full of fast Alphas in a
monster NT SMP configuration.  You can run NT on a $999 PC from a discount
store to a top of the line x86 or Alpha workstation or server.  What more could
you want?

IBM once owned the world despite technical weakness and arrogance.  They lost
it when the market moved and they didn't.  So far, Gates has kept his vision on
the market.  If Java developers and users demand platform independence and they
get decent support for Beans and OpenDoc soon, it will put Microsoft to the
test.  Or Microsoft might slip enough ActiveX into Visual J++ to kill Java's
platform independence.

Tony Scandora, Argonne National Lab, 630-252-7541
