Rich Lafferty <lafferty@cs.mcgill.ca> wrote:
> On Thu, 16 Jan 1997, Doug Cain wrote:
>> Rich Lafferty <lafferty@cs.mcgill.ca> wrote:
>> I lived with many OS's before I was exposed to VMS at v3.?? and I 
>> thought at the time It was a quantum leap past the older 
>> DEC,IBM,DG,Univac, and CDC stuff. Then I hit UNIX and thought what
>> a step backwards. Lets face it the only reason Unix became popular
>> was it was low cost (at the start, in the university environment) and
>> a generation of students got exposed to it and nothing else.
>
> Nothing else? Either you are naive, sir, or ignorant. I challenge you
> to prove to me that ARPAnet and later the Internet proper chose
> Unix-based systems because of low cost and a bunch of university
> hackers.
>
  Actually they did. After the work on TCP/IP began, the DoD released
  the sources they had to the public, so the universities and the
  general public could work & experiment with it, and thus gain from
  the experience and critique of others.

  Berkely took the sources and integrated them into BSD unix with the
  sockets interface, while AT&T added them into AT&T unix with a
  different interface - TLI.

  The DoD meant do have TCP/IP free, so as to have it more widely
  exposed, and thus UNIX & TCP/IP became such an aliance.

  If you want references to were I read this, mail me and I'll give
  you the names of the books.

 Uri Raz.





