In article <32E7C209.7D3E@videotron.ca>, Jean-Francois Mezei <"nospam<-jfmezei"@videotron.ca> writes:
>Dec is trying to convince us to run Microsoft
>applications on VMS with its "affinity" program.

Not exactly.  They are trying to convince you that such an ability exists
without bothering to tell you that the license to create such applications
comes from Bristol Technology and costs $10000/seat (single registered
user) and that each end user application copy needs an end user license,
also from Bristol Technology, currently on special for $65 a pop.  Digital
does ship the libraries needed to run such applications with newer OpenVMS 
versions, but NOT the license needed to let those libraries execute.  Evil,
really evil.  They make it sound like it is as integrated as POSIX, but it 
isn't.  It costs an arm and a leg for anybody to produce such applications
for OpenVMS, and each copy starts out with at least a $65/unit cost
disadvantage versus the Windows/WNT versions.  Oh yeah, if you want to put
PAKs on the product in addition to the Bristol Technology PAK, the PAK
generator also costs $10000 and only runs on a VAX. 

Moreover, they are trying to convince you that there actually is some
"affinity" between OpenVMS and WNT because there is now a tool to let you 
build applications on both from the same source code, when in fact the tool
that provides the ability to run WNT/Windows code on OpenVMS is, in fact,
the exact same tool that is used to run that code on Unix and Mac boxes,
and has been running there for years, only recently to be ported to
OpenVMS.  That is, there is no special affinity between WNT and OpenVMS 
when it comes to the Wind/U applications - you could just as easily build
on several other nonWNT platforms.

"Affinity" is a smoke screen, nonsense, bullshit, and those are the kinder
terms.  The SEC and FTC might consider it intentional mis- or
disinformation (I know that I do, especially Digital's pronouncements about
the Wind/U product.)  Affinity is largely just a label to hang on a bunch
of tools that were already around, and which cost so much that it is hard
to see how any but the largest organizations could possibly afford them. 
It sounds great, but see if you can actually make a case for spending the 
money for them.

Note, this is not meant in any way to impugn the operation of the Wind/U
product from Bristol Technology - I'm sure it works as they say it does.
Moreover, it is only on Bristol Technology's web pages that you will find
out what the license conditions are for Wind/U.  The mis-/disinformation is
purely a Digital product :-(.

I can't afford the Wind/U development pieces, and couldn't afford to pay at
least $65/copy for each application that I built with it. Neither can most
of you. Digital presumably knows that.  By claiming that we can now
build applications for both WNT and OpenVMS from the same code without
mentioning the high costs of doing so, they are apparently making an
intentional attempt to mislead us. If somebody at Digital would care to
comment on how/why Digital's affinity advertising/public announcements
concerning Wind/U does NOT mislead as I've just described, I'd LOVE to hear
it. 

Regards,

David Mathog
mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 



