In article <1997Jan22.191311@news.all-net.net>, byer@mail.all-net.net (Robert Byer) writes:

:I got a used VAXServer-3300 and all was fine, it booted and ran just fine
:in my computer room.  Then I replaced a memory card in the process I had
:un-hook the little front console that plugs into the CPU card.

  Be aware that it is very easy to toast most recent electronics
  with improper handling -- a wrist-strap or similar mechanism of
  avoiding static is something that is recommended to any folks
  working on any static-sensitive electronic equipment.

:Now the system won't boot, I can't see any of the DSSI hard disks that are
:in the system.  If I do the following....
..
:So my questions is, what do I have to type into this thing to get it so it
:will boot again?

  Based on the the problem statement, I suspect the cause is not likely
  anything you can remedy via typing, it's likely a hardware problem.

:Any and all help would be appreciated.

  I'd expect to find a loose, broken, backwards, or disconnected cable
  somewhere, either a power or a signal cable.  I'd look very carefully
  at what was removed and what was then reconnected, particularly around
  the CPU bulkhead and the disks.  The KA640 CPU runs the (integrated)
  DSSI and Ethernet directly on the CPU module (that's why it's called
  `integrated' :-), not via the Q-bus.  And given that the memory you
  were working on is located in immediate proximity to the CPU and CPU
  bulkhead...  It's also possible some electronics got fried...

