Digital thinks that all software writers are the size of Microsoft with
unlimited budgets and can therefore afford these "affinity costs".
Bristol knows that only a few will actually buy this stuff for a "legacy
system" (eg: one that runs stable applications with newer apps going to
other systems) so to recoup its costs, it has to charge that much.

In all fairness,  there are some uses for this software with a few of
the large VMS shops left where upgrading the user stations would cost
more than paying for the Bristol software. Remember that only large
shops with huge "IBM mainframe" like budgets are still supposed to use
VMS as all smaller shops are supposed to migrate to UNIX or NT due to
the much lower costs...

As far as the marketing stuff.

Remember when "Open" systems was the rage for a few months, and DEC
marketing decided to confuse everyone and rename VMS to oPeNvMs ?????

Of course, that "Open" fad was short lived and we don't hear about it
anymore.
Now, the rage is Windows, WIN32 and NT. So Digital wants to make sure
that it can associate these keywords with VMS in some way, even if it
doesn't mean much.

So, perhaps Digital paid a Bristol programmer a few thousand dollars to
compile that WIN stuff (which was already done for UNIX) on VMS, and
that money came from a marketing budget.



