ou006323 <kurt@deepsouth.co.nz> wrote in article <5bk95i$17a$1@celebrian.otago.ac.nz>...
> I found a VAXstation 3100 the other day and I would like a few pointers
> to get started.  I have only had brief experience with VMS and I would
> like to use this VAXstation to learn a little more.
> 
> Heres what happens when I switch it on.
> 
> It says KA42 - A V1.3
> 
> Than it displays F ... down through A ... down through the digits 1 to
> 9 with 3 dots between.  The digits 4 3 and 2 have underscores after
> them and the digit 1 has a ? after it.
> 
> There are a few more lines of garbage and then the prompt
> >>>
> I can type in help here and it gave me a list of commands like boot and
> login.  login gave me a cryptic message so I tried boot.  It displayed
> a code for about five minutes and then went back to the prompt with an
> error.
> 
> Can someone please give me some pointers to help me get this computer
> up and running?  Please point me to information available online such
> as a FAQ or something.
> 
> Thanks so much
> 
> Kurt Hausler
> 

The letters F through A and then 9 through 1 indicate self testing status'.
The question mark indicates some type of problem.  This is all documented
in the VaxStation 3100 hardware manual.  You might have a problem with
self test 1, but I don't recall what it is testing.

Entering 'Boot' by itself at the  >>> prompt assumes a default boot device
has been established.  You probably need to include a boot device on your
boot command, such as:   BOOT DKA200:    or   BOOT DKA300:
if the SCSI id of the boot disk is set to 2 or 3.  You obviously need a bootable
disk to boot from.  SCSI Id of 2 or 3 are the typical SCSI Ids for internal disks
on VaxStation 3100s, but they could be anywhere from 0 through 6.



