It might be interesting to know the memory sizes of these machines.

Page faults on VMS take a lot of time and could easily slow the machine
way down. Even mistuning (wsmax or process quotas too low) giving
soft page faults STILL mean you're timing PALcode, not yours.

Get these right and an Alpha looks a lot better, though the P6
is faster than the early Alphas. Compiler switches also make a heck
of a lot of difference, and if you do a lot of byte or short integer
operations, that could kill you too. Also note Alpha doesn't have
an integer divide (at least not 64/64). Lots of physics code (including
mine) has hand optimizations which may not work well on Alphas of
older type. The EV56 and up have byte and word instructions and will
not be so sensitive to that stuff. (I think sqrt may get in there too
as an instruction).

In short, not enough info exists in the postings to tell what is wrong,
but anyone familiar with OVMS knows it is relatively easy to mistune
it to make it look terrible. Braindead, but possible.

(Set the system working set too low and overall performance gets REALLY
bad. Autogen was partly invented to keep this from being such a problem.
But things like wsmax if you never feedback are harder to get right.
)
glenn Everhart



