  The CDC 6400, 6500, 6600, 6700, Cyber 72, Cyber 73 & Cyber 74 all had the
capabilities of up to 20 PPUs.  The 2 that you are discussing are most 
likely the chassis' that housed the PPU's.  Most standard systems were
delivered with 10 PPU's however there were several that were sold with
less.  

  Each of the PPU's was given the opportunity to execute 1 instruction
per cycle.  This was because of the memory cycle time.  The exact same
memory stack was used (1 per PPU) Part number if your interested is
10358900..  The PPU's were said to be in the barrel.  Each PPU would
upon entering the barrel SLOT time execute (or continue on the same
instruction because some instructions took multiple trips thru the
barrel to complete).  The RNI portion of the instruction took place
at approx 2 slot times prior to entering the SLOT.  The data was
sent to the central memory thru the "Write pyramid" and brought to
the PPU's thru the "Read Pyramid".  The 60 bit word was built or
broken thru the pyramid in 5 groups of 12 bits.  

  Most of this information is from memory.  I spent a number of 
years as a CE for CDC working the the Iron X.  A fantastic machine
for its' time, simple and fast..

Ed 
