In article <5bk95i$17a$1@celebrian.otago.ac.nz>, kurt@deepsouth.co.nz (ou006323) writes:
|>I found a VAXstation 3100 the other day and I would like a few pointers
|>to get started.  I have only had brief experience with VMS and I would
|>like to use this VAXstation to learn a little more.
|>
|>Heres what happens when I switch it on.
|>
|>It says KA42 - A V1.3
|>
|>Than it displays F ... down through A ... down through the digits 1 to
|>9 with 3 dots between.  The digits 4 3 and 2 have underscores after
|>them and the digit 1 has a ? after it.
|>
|>There are a few more lines of garbage and then the prompt

Those lines might not be garbage ... try posting them.

|>>>>
|>I can type in help here and it gave me a list of commands like boot and
|>login.  login gave me a cryptic message so I tried boot.  It displayed
|>a code for about five minutes and then went back to the prompt with an
|>error.
|>
|>Can someone please give me some pointers to help me get this computer
|>up and running?  Please point me to information available online such
|>as a FAQ or something.
|>


The command you want, assuming you have an OS on the disk, is

	BOOT xxxx

where xxxx is the device. SHOW DEVICE will show you the devices. You can pick
a DKA unit (internal disk), DKB unit (external disk) or ESA0 (to boot over 
the ethernet from another VMS host).

A single question mark indicates a minor problem, two question marks is 
something bad enough that you probably won't be able to boot. An underscore
means not tested (usually because it's not there).

4 is a GPX or SPX graphics card - you presumably don't have one.
3 and 2 are reserved (i.e. not implemented).
1 is the ethernet connection. Since you have a ? against this you probably 
either do not have a terminator installed or you have it on the ThinWire
connection but are using the AUI (or vice-versa).

This should not stop a boot. Try booting from a disk and post *all* of the 
resulting text (if it fails :-). Try all of the disks!

