Alex A Sergejew (aas@brain.physics.swin.oz.au) wrote:
: I need some guidance to jumper settings for a KZQSA Q-bus SCSI interface,
: and specifically on how to set this interface to a secondary address.
: I'd also very much appreciate an indication of whether this interface with
: the Ultrix 4.x SCSI driver supports SCSI-2 drives, and confirmation that
: the SCSI driver for the KZQSA does not support a boot device off this
: interface.

: The host system is a microVAX 3300, currently running Ultrix 4.0, with
: a KA640-AA CPU, MS650-BA and MS650-AA memory boards, and TKQ70 interface
: (boot config log appended below).  The system appears to have a built-in
: DSSI interface at the primary disk address 174500, and is working fine with
: three DSSI disk drives off it.  I have just acquired an M5976 (KZQSA) interface
: with a view to adding SCSI disks to the system (eg as the DSSI drives die)
: but have no KZQSA documentation at all.

: I think I will need to jumper the KZQSA to a secondary disk address, and
: would be very grateful for any advice.  Currently, W 3, 4, 10, 14, 15, and 16
: are jumpered.

I think you are trying to make this interface a SCSI 'target', when it is usually
used as a 'host' or 'initiator'.  I have some doubt that it can do this, due
solely to driver limitations, probably.  It is fairly likely that it could do
this with a custom driver for this purpose.  The address of the controller is
probably set by software (driver) not some physical hardware jumpers.  Those jumpers
probably set CSR address, and perhaps interrupt vector.

: I would also appreciate it if some kind soul could advise me whether the
: KZQSA can handle ordinary generic (non-DEC) SCSI-2 disk drives.

: Finally, I've just noticed that the Ultrix 4.3 scsi (4) man page states:
: 	Restrictions:
: 	Devices on the KZQSA interface cannot be used as boot devices.
: and I would appreciate having this restriction confirmed or otherwise...

This is almost certainly another driver restriction, not a hardware restriction.
I can't see any reason non-dec devices can't be used, except again, driver
limitations.  That's what you get from Ultrix.  A BSD driver, if it supported
the KZQSA, would have no difficulty supporting compatible devices, although
there might be a size limit on disks.
					Jon




