"For whatever reason".  How well put, Tony.

In the long run, the result of many companies buying software
architected by marketers instead of engineers is that many companies
will go out of business.

The problem is simply to define "the long run".

Microsoft has some wonderful engineers.  Unfortunately, they're the
tail, not the dog.  It is necessary to listen to the market.  Alas,
_the market doesn't always know what it needs_.  Note that I said
_needs_.  Customers often buy what the bosses like, regardless of what
the engineers charged with doing the _work_ tell them is required. 

(Ghod knows I understand this problem; Hal Hassel, may you burn in hell)

:                                Look at UNIX.  Most UNIX systems today don't
: even run a layered product capable of providing batch and print queue services
: inferior to what was in VMS V1.0, and UNIX security lets almost anyone become
: root via sendmail bugs and countless programs that let long command lines dance
: on the stack,

Funny, my _free_ copy of Linux doesn't seem to have all these problems.
Again, commercial versions of Unix suffer from the same syndrome.

:                  yet UNIX has overtaken VMS' share of the enterprise.  UNIX
: clusters don't have block-served disks or a distributed lock manager, but the
: add-on Oracle Parallel Server protocol allows Oracle's database engine to take
: advantage of clustering.

I gather this is related to Async IO.  (Which Linux is also capable
of now.)

:                             The operating system is simply no longer the most
: important part of the system.  The Oracle and Informix databases and the mail
: and groupware services are how the enterprises see their systems, and the
: computer people earn their paychecks by doing whatever it takes to keep the
: Oracle and Informix databases and the mail and groupware services reliable and
: available.

If they can.  The platform is not the _most_ important poart, no... but
how many Oracle7 database systems do you see running on DOS boxen?

: NT V4.0 and SQL Server V6.5 are not even close to enterprise worthy.  

Glad we agree on this.

: As Digital's marketing destroyed VMS and almost the company by flagrant
: incompetence, Microsoft's marketing will keep the heat on the products until
: the market buys millions of them.  They needed cluster technology, so they
: signed a deal with Digital.  Once they get all the cluster technology they
