> >> campbejr@phu989.mms.sbphrd.com (John R. Campbell) writes:
> >> 
> >4> Instructions were 15/30/60 bit so you could have up to 4 instructions
> >> per word.  
> 
> You haven't mentioned that there were no load or store instructions, either.
> Changing a value of a B register (I think) would load/store the word from
> memory whose address was in the B register into/from the corresponding (e.g.,
> B3 corresponds to A3) A (?) register.  

It was the A registers (18 bits) that were paired with the X registers (60
bits).  There were also B registers (18 bits).   There were 8 of each.

There were no load or store instructions, as mentioned, setting the value
of an A register caused something to happen to the corresponding X register.
A1-A5 caused X1-X5 to be loaded from memory, A6-A7, caused X6-X7 to be stored
to memory.  A0/X0 had no connection.

The B registers were index registers.  B0 was always permanently 0.
This allowed the same instruction to take one parameter (an A B or X
register) or 2 parameters (the sum of a B register and one of an A B or X
register)

(exercise: From this, develop a sequence that saves or loads ALL registers
to/from memory given the above.  It's harder than it looks!)

