In article <32fe1d80.1109477744@news.sci.fi> keinanen@sci.fi (Paul Kein�nen) writes:


  > I doubt that any automatic voice recognition system would be
  > much better. BTW, VMS command language is quite good compared to many
  > other command languages _if_ the other person knows English quite
  > well, however, if you have to spell out each command character by
  > charecter, some other command line might be easier.
  > 
  > Just wondering what it would require to use voice recognition for
  > unix, appearantly you have to spell out each character in phonetic
  > alphabet (alfa, beta, charlie, delta, etc.).
  > 

Well, it wouldn't be much different from the VMS examples.
How would you spell a command like 
DIR DISK$USER:[USERS...]*NAME*.COM
Typing is much faster, I guess.

In general, I do not think that a voice recognition "shell" would
just replace the keyboard. Rather than imitating the shell commands
it needs an additional step beyond these, like a GUI interface which
replaces some shell commands.

In a traditional GUI interface, you wouldn't try to directly
implement every switch and option with some extra pushbutton or option menu,
just to make it "clickable".
Think about how a "file manager" application looks like, and
what the shell commmands behind this are.
You get a frequently used subset of the available operations, offered
in a more or less intuitive and user friendly way.

A voice recognition system would "look" quite similar, I think.
But I wouldn't want one - I just imagined my workmate sitting next to me,
chatting to his computer all the time while I'm trying to concentrate
or while I have to phone... no thanks.

Greetings,
  Christian

