| It was the A registers (18 bits) that were paired with the X registers 
| (60 bits).  There were also B registers (18 bits).   There were 8 of 
| each.
| 
| ...[S]etting the value of an A register caused something to happen to 
| the corresponding X register. A1-A5 caused X1-X5 to be loaded from 
| memory, A6-A7, caused X6-X7 to be stored to memory.  A0/X0 had no 
| connection.
|
| The B registers were index registers....
|
| (exercise: From this, develop a sequence that saves or loads ALL 
| registers to/from memory given the above.  It's harder than it looks!)

Actually, it's not just *harder* than it looks - as posed, the problem
can't be solved!  (There *is* a solution, but it involves knowing that
the subroutine call instruction on the 6600 planted the return address -
well, a *junp* to the return address - at the beginning of the routine
being called.)

BTW, while many people know the "save all registers" question, not many
realize that *restoring* all registers is at least as hard!  (You can't
do it using the parts of the instruction set defined so far either....)

(And even once you figure out the trick - which involves one of the
rarely-used FP instructions - you *still* will probably find that your
code restores -0 incorrectly!  That's easy to fix - but you have to
think of it.)
							-- Jerry



