> left three words, kill word, input mode, the, command mode, right three
> words" can be faster than typing ^A^A^A^Tthe^F^F^F. Perhaps they'll come
> out with a decent way to coordinate voice, keyboard, and mouse -- but I
> have little faith that they will, and it sure seems like a waste if you
> enjoy typing and are aware that written language is very different from
> spoken language. Maybe I'll give it the old college try anyway -- as I
> tried GUIs, now long gone from my hard disk....

My fiancee talks of the time when computer programmers (late 70's) thought
that non programmers should not own computers.  While my views are not that
extreme, I do think GUIs need to be rethought.  It is the stupidest thing
that half the time I type something in, I have to use my mouse to click
"enter".  There is a reason we have a key labeled "ENTER" right?

The fact is, I type 70+ words a minute.  It's a hell of a lot faster for me
to type in commands than point and click.  I also don't have to look at the
screen and rely ony my hand-eye coordination to get something done.  What's
next?  A GUI word processor with the letters arrayed on the screen and
pretty buttons that you have to point and click to get a letter?  Will
people brag about there mousing speed?  5WPM?  Most savvy people I know
really like Norton Commander.  Couldn't they integrate something like that?
 Have icons maybe for file copy, but a command line just as potent
underneath?  (Not just a Windows '95 "Oh yeah--there are shortcut keys...
somewhere).

Thanks for letting me rant.

