> >On Wed, 15 Jan 1997 16:39:11 -0500, jfmezei <nospam.jfmezei@videotron.ca> wrote:
> 
> >>GUI is good for desktop stuff (word processing, charts, drawings or any
> >>other stuff that should look good). But GUI is not as good for managing
> >>systems where command-line is more productive to those who know it well.
> 
> >Just why IMHO GUI should be left to the realm of applications where
> >it can make a difference, and not to the realm of system administration.
> 
> Last time I heard that argument somebody was trying to tell me that
> secretaries were more productive using Wordperfect 4.2.
> 
> What are you dinosaurs going to do when voice recognition becomes the
> norm?

Have you ever actually used voice recognition?  Have you seen how
annoying it is?  Do you think most people are going to put up with
having to say "open aplication", "do this", "do that", etc.  It is
slow.  Even if it was really good continuous speech recognition, it
would be *much* slower than using A GUI.

...Of course, using a GUI is much slower than actually doing things at
the command line... even for file management.  It's just that you have
more learning to do with the command line interface.  When you learn it,
it is wonderful.
(Of course, I mean a *good* command line interface such as the UNIX
prompt, and not COMMAND.COM on MS-DOS.  I'd always rather use a GUI than
that thing.)

Even things that have to look good can be faster without the GUI.  For
example, I've seen equations typeset in AmSTeX, and I've seen the
equation editors found in most word processors.  The equation editors
are much worse.

The *only* thing going against command line interfaces is the learning
curve. 

