Where straight writing and editing are concerned, WordPerfect is almost
as bad as current GUIs. Luckily, almost all its keystrokes can be
changed. The only thing that cuts the mustard for this full-time writer
(and many others) is WordStar.
 
> What are you dinosaurs going to do when voice recognition becomes the
> norm?

The prospect of an office full of people muttering to their machines does
not appeal to me, especially as I live in a society where few ever think
to keep their voices down. And I fail to see how saying "command mode,
left three words, kill word, input mode, the, command mode, right three
words" can be faster than typing ^A^A^A^Tthe^F^F^F. Perhaps they'll come
out with a decent way to coordinate voice, keyboard, and mouse -- but I
have little faith that they will, and it sure seems like a waste if you
enjoy typing and are aware that written language is very different from
spoken language. Maybe I'll give it the old college try anyway -- as I
tried GUIs, now long gone from my hard disk....

