lstowell@pyrtech.mis.pyramid.com (Lon Stowell) writes:

>  Your machine has the option of a nice graphic display of all of
>  its activity, or you could choose to pour thru 250K lines of
>  sar output to narrow down the problem and resolve it.  You
>  have one hour to come up with the correct answer.    

What you don't understand is that the odds the nice graphic display will
tell you what you need to know is abysimal.  Humans are better pattern
matchers than machines; with a graphical display, it could take you
forever just to figure out which setting describes the problem best.  I
can make repeated analyses of the sar output with perl, awk, even grep
and get all the information I need.  Heck, I can even do a primitive
strip chart if that's what I need.  Thus, in my hour I might have to do
some puzzling over the sar output, but at least I have all the info I
need right there when I need it.

I currently have a load average indicator and some kernel info
displayed on my monitor.  While this is fine to tell me there is a
problem, I'll need to run top, ps or the like to figure out that my
ghostscript job is hogging about 30% of my swap space.  There is no way
any amount of graphics can contain the information in a single line of
sar output, let alone 250K lines.

As a final passing note, let's say you're in Bermuda at the time, and
the Big Mean Boss wants it fixed _now_.  Can you watch your nice pretty
display remotely?  I can grep through sar output just like I'm there.
