In article <32E54D60.58E6EAC@undergrad.math.uwaterloo.ca>, Boris Gjenero <bgjenero@undergrad.math.uwaterloo.ca> writes:
>> What are you dinosaurs going to do when voice recognition becomes the
>> norm?
>
>Have you ever actually used voice recognition?  Have you seen how
>annoying it is?  Do you think most people are going to put up with
>having to say "open aplication", "do this", "do that", etc.  It is
>slow.  Even if it was really good continuous speech recognition, it
>would be *much* slower than using A GUI.

We are getting a bit off subject here, but here goes.

1.  Most VMS commands would be much easier to work into a voice recognition
system than would a GUI interface or a Unix command line. The OpenVMS
command line syntax is largely consistent between commands and the language
is a sort of twisted English.  Consider a set of simple but common tasks. 

  $ dir/since=yesterday
  $ copy file1.txt file2.txt

How many words would that take?

  dir slash since equal yesterday GO
  copy file number 1 dot t x t space file number 2 GO

Potentially one could use the CLD information to intelligently parse a
spoken command line to cut down on the number of words, here those that
would be implicit and could be left out are in {}

  dir {slash} since {equal} yesterday GO

(Because these are invalid:

   $ dir since yesterday
   $ dir since/yesterday

 This is valid, but might be ruled invalid if there was a rule that
 command parsing always resolved towards the use of keywords unless 
 explicit slash/space/dots were used. 

   $ dir/since yesterday
)

Notice that there is big problem with ambiguities in the recognized speech
that are essentially unresolvable (short of HAL level AI). How does the
interface recognize that the file is "file2" and not "fileto", "filetwo",
or "filetoo"?  We also had to spell "txt" since saying "text" would have
added the extra E.  Presumably if you worked all the time on such a system
you would use "file.text".  Again, somefields would be known to be numeric
and so not be ambiguous

  $ type file.text;54
        =
  type file {dot} text {version} fifty four GO

2.  Case sensitivity at arbitrary positions is nearly fatal in a speech
recognition system, think what fun it would be getting this through 
correctly:

   zip -L ThisDamnCaseSensitivity.zip *

>The *only* thing going against command line interfaces is the learning
>curve. 

3.  In a voice recognition system you *still* have to learn the commands
and you *still* have to learn the syntax.  In that sense it is identical to
a command line interface.  While you can argue that a voice recognition
system would respond to "Computer, how do I do x?" that is little different
than typing HELP at the command line or clicking on the HELP button.  
"Natural language voice recognition" is even harder than "natural language"
command line recognition.  If you have the option of seeing the screen
while you work, then the voice recognition system could build the command
on the screen for you, so that you could tell when it was confused. Somehow
I doubt that would be faster than just typing in the command. 

4.  Where voice recognition would be really useful is in the preliminary
transcription of long pieces of arbitrary speech or carefully delimited
data entry.  Lots of people compose better when speaking than they do by
typing or writing with a pen.  Voice recognition would also be helpful in
those extremely limited domains where there are few commands and little
syntax.  For instance, in a car, "turn on the motor authorization XYZ",
"turn on the wipers", "turn off the wipers", "clean the windshield".  Maybe
20 short phrases would cover everything you normally tell a car to do.  I'd
still want a brake peddle and a steering wheel though! 

Regards,

David Mathog
mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 



