This whole debate about GUI, voice recognition and command line forces
me to say the following:

As new technologies come out, some companies try to make such
technologies pervasive and often exagerate.

When the MAC came out, they went overboard with GUI and completely left
out a command line interface. They have since realised their mistake and
now have apple-script.

When Apple introduced voice recognition on the MACs, (yes, you can set
it up to say "open document" etc), it didn't quite catch on.

When Apple introduced handwriting recognition on the NEWTON, it didn't
quite catch on.

However, each of these human interfaces have good potential uses where
they are efficient, but are NOT EFFICIENT EVERYWHERE.

Speech recognition is very useful in manufacturing where a worker can
shout "STOP" and the machine stops. It is useful (although I hate it)
for telephony). But it is not useful on desktop computers (unless you do
not have full use of your hands).

GUI is useful for office work where "computer illiterate" folks are
exepected to use a tool to write documents, print them and now e-mail
them.

Command-line interface still has its uses where it is more efficient
than GUI or speech recognition.


In my opinion, a good system is one which recognises the advantages of
command line and the advantages of GUI and uses both to their advantage.



