>> On balance, though, I believe that maintaining passwords in a text file,
>> even encrypted, is a very bad idea.

>   1. I would like to note, as done by others before me, that DECnet phase IV
>      has kept the passwords for the DECnet objects accounts in a file, and
>      relied on proper protections to prevent the passwords from leakage.

Of course I would not want to set a precedent for these newsgroups
by criticizing some action taken by DEC :-), but the original DECnet
for VMS (phase II ?) had a lot more of this password storage within
DECnet.  By the time Phase IV rolled around, if you were using the
documented and recommended setup techniques (rather than the older
techniques still supported for the sake of compatibility), the only
passwords stored by DECnet on a machine where for that machine itself,
relying on no machine to guard the security of another machine.

Still for some of us, that is not enough, considering for instance
the typical habit of using unencrypted backup tapes.  DEC apparently
agrees, as they have avoided this practice for DECnet Phase V/OSI/Plus.
Of course that implementation still does not support node-specific
authentication restrictions (SET EXECUTOR DEFAULT ACCESS NONE), and
some of us wish they had hired a few more programmers and a few less
product namers.

> kilgallen@eisner.decus.org (Larry Kilgallen) wrote:

>> DEC Encryption for VMS can handle this, but it is export controlled
>> from the US.

<snip reference to Bruce Schneier's excellent book>

> Which reminds me - now that there is a BACKUP API in OpenVMS, does anybody
> know if there is a way to create encrypted backup using a private encryption
> utility (or routines), rather then DEC's ENCRYPT ?

Even before the BACKUP API, I would think someone willing to write
their own could just write a replacement for DEC's encryption module.
A dummy version is shipped with VMS.  The calling conventions are not
published, presumably because that would have made all of VMS become
"ancillary cryptographic apparatus" in the view of US NSA officials
and thus not generally exportable.

Larry Kilgallen



