The entire source code is several thousand lines long, and I am not
about to post it or give it out just yet. I originally designed the
program
for the CM5, where I achieved peak flops rates near 100Mflops/node
(which
was pretty good in its day, on the CM5). When I started to port the
code over to the T3D, I first got a version to run on my pentium, since
I decided to use ANSI C... anyway, I made an initial crude version,
and it performed very well on the pentium, and not so well on an alpha.

The thing about lattice QCD is that it uses standard iterative methods
such as conjugate gradient, etc, on large linear systems, BUT the
elements
of the "matrices" are not numbers, but rather complex 3x3 matrices 
themselves. The basic operation in the code is the following
(corresponding 
to a simple multiplication in a normal CG routine, for example):

dest_vector = src_matrix*src_vector

(although there are several variants, such as adding the result
to another vector, etc...)

where the matrices are 3x3 and complex, and the vectors are
complex 3 vectors.... all variables being of type "float". There are
many ways to perform such an operation efficiently, and usually I would
attempt to "vectorize" it (so that the compilers can work their magic,
I suppose). It just so happens that the most obvious and
crudest (and my initial) attempt performed exceptionally well on a 
pentium, and poorly on an alpha.

Try it out for yourself.

I must admit I made no attempt to make it run fast on an alpha, I was
just
surprised to see the pentium outperform the alphas I used on the first
"draft".



