You also might want to take a look at my somewhat optimized
Reed-Solomon implementation that comes as part of the Linux
floppy-tape driver.  Last time I measured it, the Alpha vastly
outperformed anything else I had access to at the time (that was a
couple of years ago).  The interesting thing was that the
Alpha-optimized version was faster on all RISC machines I had access
to (PA-RISC, SPARC, MIPS) but ran a bit slower on the 486.

If anybody is interested in looking at it, give me a holler and I'll
dig out the sources.  If you have a reasonably recent Linux kernel
source tree (e.g., 2.0), you'll find the actual RS code in:

	linux/drivers/char/ftape/ecc.{c,h}

I do have a performance & correctness test program for that code, but it
doesn't come with the Linux sources, so you'd have to ask me for that one.

