organization: The University of Arizona, Tucson, AZ
newsgroups: comp.sys.intel,comp.sys.dec


Just a quick update: I located the Reed-Solomon code I mentioned in a
previous post and measured it on a few machines I have access to.  The
numbers below are the average Reed-Solomon encoding/decoding
throughputs for the code that is used by floppy-tape drives:

 AXPpci33 (166MHz 21066, i.e., _very_ slow memory system): 20328KB/sec
 P6 200MHz (Gateway, I believe)				 : 28438KB/sec
 Cabriolet (300MHz 21064A)				 : 40860KB/sec
 eb164 (300MHz 21164)					 : 62301KB/sec

The AXPpci33 is my personal machine that can be bought for $700 or so
under the name Multia (or UDB), so it's not exactly a high end machine
(and normally it's a lot slower than a P6 because of its crappy memory
system).  The Cabriolet is also known as AlphaPC64 is quite old.  The
eb164 is a reasonably new machine, though it doesn't have any of the
sub-word store/load features of the newer CPUs.

It's interesting to note that at least for the Alphas, RS encoding
throughput seems to be almost exactly half of the copy-bandwidth of
the memory system.

Just goes to show: lies, damn lies, and benchmarks.

	--david

PS: If anybody is interested in looking at the code: I put together a
    tar-file with all the necessary bits and the code is known to compile
    at least under Linux for Alpha and x86.  I'm sure there is room for
    improvement for both the Alpha and the x86 given that the code was written
    3 or 4 years ago.
-- 
