David Mosberger-Tang wrote:
> 
> janes@signal.dra.hmg.gb (Tim W. Janes) writes:
> > Here are execution time comparisons for a run of our in house speech
> > recognition code.
> >
> > PPro 200 (NetBSD)         127 secs
> > Alphastation 600 5/266 (DU) 134 secs
> > Alphastation 500 5/333 (DU) 109 secs
> >
> > I had put down the relative good PPro performance to the fact that all
> > published performance figures use 64bit floats but we use 32Bit floats
> > and a PPro does 32bit arithmetic very much faster than 64 but an alpha
> > does 64 and 32 bit arithmetic at the same speed.
> >
> > Sorry DEC but at the moment we are very happy with the
> > performance/cost of our array of cheap PPro machines.
> 
> Assuming your code is written in C and you were using DEC C on the DU
> machines: did you make sure that -std1 was turned on when using the
> DEC C compiler?  Without it, the compiler defaults to K&R mode which
> has the effect that all fp operations are performed in double
> precision (and since you're using floats, that would result in many
> unneeded conversions).  In contrast, gcc defaults to ANSI mode.

I recompiled my code with all the compiler options suggested to me,
including the -std1 switch you mentioned (this has the greatest effect)
and -tune ev5, and I can now improve the alpha numbers:

before optimization: 333MHz alphastation 500 : 76 MFlops/s
after optimization                           : 89 Mflops/s

This is still slower than the 200MHz ppro, at 105 Mflops/s.



