In article <32E04B55.588B@jtc.gov.sg>, Chen Shu Quan <shuquan@jtc.gov.sg> writes:

:Currently, we maintain a list of usernames and passwords in a text file,
:and are looking for some utilities to encrypt and decrypt this file.

   Why do you even have unencrypted passwords saved on disk? 

   I'd strongly suggest using the one-way encyrption scheme used by
   OpenVMS and various other platforms -- this one-way scheme greatly
   reduces the exposure of this information.  And in this case, it
   would tend to avoid -- in conjunction with a good password choice
   policy -- the en-mass compromising of passwords...

   OpenVMS provides the sys$hash_password interface -- when performing
   a password comparison, one hashes the proposed password value and
   compares it against the saved hashed value.

   In any case, this file should also be marked for erase-on-delete,
   and should be heavily protected and audited against attack.

:Any utility available on VMS, pls let me know.

   Yes, Digital offers an encryption layered product.

   Most reliable encryption algorythms are presently export-controlled
   munitions, and the exportation (and sometimes the importation) of
   munitions usually requires a license.  (The US presently requires
   an export license for Digital's -- and other -- encryption products.
   There are presently various governmental and commercial discussions
   over this requirement/restriction occuring here in the US.)

