Gene Wirchenko wrote:
> 
> Ron Natalie <ron@sensor.com> wrote:

> >We won't talk about the night I got to play operator on the CDC
> >systems and the carnage that resulted...
> 
>      Oh, come on, "Uncle" Ron.  Tell us a bedtime story.

I was primarily a communications person at the time.  We had
a massive overnight effort to rip out a zillion RS-232 lines that
snaked from various MUX's and modems and route them through
a new Gandalf Port Selector.  This night we were doing the
Cyber.

Well we were told (perhaps just a rumor) that unplugging the
terminal lines could crash the cyber, so the operators shut
down the "network" (the part of the system that talks to
the terminals) but left it running.  Long about midnight,
the operator decides he isn't going to stay around anymore
so opens up a lineprinted operations manual and plops a tape
on it to hold it open and then says start typing here.

Problem was that he didn't tell me where to stop and after
about ten pages, I gave up.  The next morning they asked
me what I had done, and I told them I had no idea.  Evidentally,
jobs kept migrating between the front ends and it took days
to get them all killed.

-Ron



