In article <greg.853523214@gtravis> greg@indiana.edu (Gregory Travis) writes:

| The technology that made this all possible was the "checkpoint" facility.
| 
| Taking a checkpoint of a job produced a file which was, essentially, the
| job's entire state.  Almost exactly like a Unix "core" file.  The job could
| be restarted from this checkpoint file.  I believe Unix Emacs used to do
| something almost exactly like this - it produced a "temacs" executable from
| its own core dump.

On Kronos at least, it also was an incredible security hole, since the
checkpoint of a job included the privilege bits.  You could checkpoint a job,
twiddle the security bits, and restart it.  Instant superuser!  At least at the
University of Minnesota, they disabled user checkpoints for this reason.

Gnu based emacsen on most platforms still create the emacs from temacs.  Temacs
loads many of the lisp files and writes out the emacs binary with the lisp heap
area initialized to the current contents.  Unlike some versions of TeX, emacs
does this by writing the new executable itself, not by pawing through the core
file.
-- 
