From article <greg.853528950@gtravis>, by greg@indiana.edu (Gregory Travis):
> 
> Ugh, who else remembers PR1MOS?  Was there anything right about that
> operating system?

In the end, there was everything right about PR1MOS.  Somewhere around the
middle of the lifetime of PR1MOS, Prime hired some multicians, and as the
end of Prime approaches, PR1MOS became more and more MULTICS from the user
viewpoint.  It had ACL's done right, not kluged on the way UNIX systems do,
and Prime PL/I was a clean version of the language!  The University of Hull
had a great Pascal compiler for the Prime, and while I was glad when we
replace them with UNIX boxen at Iowa, I do miss some features.

Early PR1MOS, however, was a mess, and if you descended to the instruction
set level, you face the world's most confused instruction set, save one.
(That one is the market leader today, which goes to show that a computer
doesn't need a good instruction set to be successful in the marketplace.)

Deep down inside, every Prime computer was actually a 3C DDP 516 -- Prime
was founded by ex 3C people who didn't like the Honeywell takeover, and as
an ex DDP 516 programmer, I found that connection to be somewhat fun.

By the way, DDP 516 Fortran was a pretty nice implementation of the
language, and the DDP 516 linking loader was a marvel -- there's nothing
quite like running on a machine with only 16K 16 bit words of core memory
and finding software of that quality.  Of corse, the way DDP sold it, it
was paper-tape based, but I was involved in patching the compiler and
loader to run from disk back in '73.

				Doug Jones
				jones@cs.uiowa.edu



