On Fri, 03 Jan 1997 21:20:52 +0000, in alt.folklore.computers, Tim
Adye <T.J.Adye@rl.ac.uk> wrote:

>My theory for why organisations so often choose to
>use/standardise on/endorse M$ stuff is that the people that actually
>make such decisions don't usually have to manage or use it.

Abso-freakin'-lutely!

A few years back, while planning a move to a new office, the person
who was working with me on facilities planning was sick and the
president of the (small) company decided to take over a meeting with a
wiring vendor.  The salesvermin was BSing his way along and, after the
meeting, the president told me that I'd been needlessly harsh with
him.  I tried to explain myself, starting with some technical details,
and he interrupted me, saying "I don't care about the details..."

I shot back, "Of course _you_ don't because, when something goes
wrong, you call _me_ to fix them!" and walked out.

In many organizations, I expect that such an action would be enough to
get one's ass canned to Hell and back, but this president has a few
attributes that made dealing with him an interesting procedure,
including the fact that only the first statement made in a discussion
has any effect on him (and even that takes time to sink in) so there's
no need to conduct a lengthy discussion... and he has grudging respect
for people who are usually right.  <grin>

>Or am I just cynical (though not cynical enough to believe its really
>down to bribery)? 

You're absolutely right, but the problem runs deeper than you state.
Time was, managers were good people promoted from the operations area
of the company and who not only understood the day to day work, but
were good at it because their experience and instinct gave them a gut
feeling that was as good or better than some people's careful
analysis.

Today, companies recruit managers from business administration
programmes at colleges and universities.  These people come into a
company not only completely ignorant of the operations and without any
feel for what will and won't work, they're trained to concentrate on
frilly things like motivation and not impediments like facts.  This
leads them to make poor decisions and then tell their employees, "We
have commitments to meet... I don't want to hear why it _can't_ be
done, I want to hear how it _can_ be done!"

The end result is that a few organizations make up for these poor
management decisions by squeezing more out of their people, but most
survive by doing poor quality work.  In both cases, the company's
'achievements' (aside of making a buck) are hollow or illusory.

This explains not only why companies hop on bandwagons like
Microsoft's, but also why the software products that we see today -
including Microsoft's operating systems - are as unreliable as they
are.

--
