In article <32E0EA69.3C56@hda.hydro.com>, Terje Mathisen
<Terje.Mathisen@hda.hydro.com> wrote:

> John A. Limpert wrote:
> [snip]
> > I benchmarked an Alpha with a Reed-Solomon encoder that I wrote and my
> > 90 MHz Pentium beat it. The Alpha seemed to have alot of trouble with
> > all of the byte sized operations in the encoder software. The Alpha is
> > a neat chip but it stumbled on my application.
> 
> That was obvious from the first time I laid eyes on the original Alpha
> Architecture Manual: With no 8 or 16-bit memory accesses, you really
> need to make sure your code doesn't need to work with many items like
> that, esp. not with unknown alignment.
> 
> This is an area which have obviously hurt DEC, since they added these
> operations to the 21264.

   They were added in the 21164, not the 264 (which isn't even sampling yet
?). Another way to look at this is "only the 21064 lacks byte and sub word
operations".

   I don't think it "hurt" them until they started porting NT and NT apps
that grew up in an Intel-centric world. Their SPEC and TPC numbers were
always quite good relative to their contemporaries, even in the 21064 days,
thanks in no small part to their compilers that really understood how to
make the most of the architecture.

