> > Question number 2, what compiler are you using on each
> > machine and what switches are you setting ?
> 
> VC++ 4.0 on the Intel machines, and plain old cc -Owhatever on the
> alpha.... same source code in both cases.

This could be part of the problem.  I've found DEC hardware to be
fairly sensitive to the compiler optimizations used, that's why
I asked.  '-Owhatever' doesn't tell me what I need to know, except
that maybe you're not using the best optimizations.

 The notebook was running windows 95.... Not bad, considering that the same 
> code runs at 24 Mflops/s on a single node of the T3D (with its "150 Mflop"
> alphas).

Actually, your code will run faster on a Win95 machine than under NT
(I'm sure you've figured this out already).

If it's short loops, then it won't run well on those machines (T3D),
since, if I'm not mistaken, they're vector machines.

> And as for someone who mentioned that I had not parallelized it well,
> it scales almost perfectly at 20mflops/node all the way up to 512
> nodes on the T3D.

That was me.  From your previous posts, it was unclear (proposefully so ?)
as to exactly what point you were trying to make.  You were commmenting
about how your PPro was faster than a 16 node T3D...

Jeff



