Terry Kennedy  <terry@spcuna.spc.edu> wrote:
>  Also, note that the *only* systems that officially supported the KDA50 all
>came with one pre-installed (the dual-BA23/RA81 configuration), so be careful.
>Not all cabinets can support this option. I've run it successfully in a BA123.

What ones didn't?  I've seen two KDA50 configurations in BA213 boxes, one
which had SDI bulkheads in the drive bay of the cabinet, the other had a
SDI bulkheads in a three slot wide backplane cover plate.  The former (a
MicroVAX 3900) system was supplied in a rack; the latter (a DECsystem
5400) is in the floorstand version of the same box, and has an internal
DSSI disk as well as external SDI disks.  (The drive bay bulkheads, front 
panel etc on the DS5400 are intended the DSSI disks, as opposed to the 
panels on the MV3900 which were intended to attach internal SDI disks.)

I imagine that a BA23 cabinet kit could be kludged into a BA213 if one 
didn't have the BA213 mounting kit on hand, if one was prepared to do a 
little metalwork.

The KDA50s on both these boxes are standard -- the only difference
between them and the BA23 versions is the cabling and bulkheads.  On BA23
& BA213 systems, the panel inserts are standard 'B' type inserts, which
doesn't leave a whole lot of room on the back of a single BA23.

The other real snag is that the KDA50 is a power hog.  The dual-BA23 
config came with the KDA50 installed on its own in the second backplane.
I'm not sure what the numbers are for the KDA50 -- my catalogues are at 
work and I'm not, and the 1985 MicroVAX II Technical Manual predates the 
beast.  I suspect that installing a KDA50 into a single BA23 is asking 
for trouble, but get the power and bus load numbers and do the sums -- if 
there's not much in the box you might get away with it.

There's some mutterings in the Jan 1990 DECdirect catalogue about BA123 
systems shipped prior to 30 Apr 1987 -- there's an extra kit required.  
Or something.  It's not very specific. 

BTW: Is there a nice list of Qbus parts including bus & power load info 
out there?  Would be real nice to have...

