Hi Ron,

Ron Natalie <ron@sensor.com> writes:

>Yep, you have to realize that the thing was pretty much a batch engine.
>All the NOS ugliness got regurgitated into COS on the Crays, familiar
>enough, but different enough, to unnerve programmers making the switch.

This is one "feature" that SCOPE/KRONOS/NOS (CDC operating systems) shared
with MVS/TSO on IBMs.  When "timesharing" (i.e. using a terminal) to the
system, the system effectively "submitted" every command that you typed
as a batch job.  Priorities, of course, were changed for timesharing-origin
jobs, etc. but deep down KRONOS couldn't tell that your typed request
for a CATALOG (UNIX equivalent of "ls") at the terminal was anything different
from a CATALOG card submitted at the cardreader.  The timesharing system
took care of inserting the right "job" cards (based on your timesharing
login) before submitting your command to the batch system.

Like IBM's MFT, Kronos had a static number of "job slots" into which active
jobs could go.  The number was a site parameter.  As I recall, a certain
number of slots were reserved for jobs submitted from the timesharing
system.  Still, if the machine was real busy, your "CATALOG" command would
go into the "waiting to be scheduled for execution" queue.  Once a job
WAS scheduled to a "Control Point" (I just rememembered the name!), it
had it for life but it could be "rolled out" (swapped) for memory or
"checkpointed" which means a snapshot was taken of its current state
and it was put back into the INPUT queue.  The latter was RARE at our site

>I always thought that Senator was a pretty neat name for the text
>editor.

We used CDC's EDIT (ugh!) and the much better XEDIT and then UCEDIT.
In the final years, one of our very good systems programmers wrote a
version of XEDIT that actually ran on a PPU.  It was INCREDIBLY fast
(for what we were used to) for little things but started to eat it if
you loaded a big file or tried a complex replacement.

>-Ron

>We won't talk about the night I got to play operator on the CDC
>systems and the carnage that resulted...

Oh, God, here I go again.  The Console on the 6600 was TRULY a "console."

It consisted of TWO absolutely round scopes and a little 64-key keyboard.

The scopes were attached to some channel, can't remember which, and could
be directly read or written by any PPU which got the channel.  The hardware
consisted of a simple vector-plotter.  There was no memory-mapped video
in those days.  The attached PPU would send commands down the channel
telling the display how to DRAW each character as a series of vectors.  As
a result, the console "font" was super-funky (it looked like my handwriting)
as sometimes the vectors would "overshoot" a little.

Also, console screen refresh was done by the PPUs and if the driving PPU
got behind (because it was busy or memory was slow or...) then the
display would start to noticibly flicker and waver.

There were a number of console PPU utility programs and they could be
attached to either scope as I recall.  A typical setup at our shop would
be to have one scope being driven by a program which displayed the "Control
Points" (active jobs) and their associated information.  The other scope
would be driven by a program which looked at various parts of memory or
manipulated SPOOL (card/print/etc.) queues.

There were some games as well - I remember "baseball" which was INCREDIBLE
given that these were the days before video arcade games.  It drew a
baseball diamond on the screen and had a bat and everything.  Also "Snoopy" in
which Snoopy flew his doghouse across the console screens.  Remember, this
is all VECTOR graphics on semi-analog display hardware.  Really gave the whole
thing a warm feel.

When we got the Cyber 172, CDC had replaced the two separate scopes with
one big screen that was divided (in hardware) into two sides.  Gross.  BUt
the vector graphics remained.

I could go on about this all day.

greg
