jmp@cocos.fuw.edu.pl (Jerzy Michal Pawlak) writes:

>The OS had some amazing properties you don't find anywhere now. For example 
>all the queues (including executing jobs) survived a deadstart (or reboot as 
>we call it today).

They only survived if the deadstart was orderly (i.e. there had been a
graceful shutdown prior to the deadstart).

The technology that made this all possible was the "checkpoint" facility.

Taking a checkpoint of a job produced a file which was, essentially, the
job's entire state.  Almost exactly like a Unix "core" file.  The job could
be restarted from this checkpoint file.  I believe Unix Emacs used to do
something almost exactly like this - it produced a "temacs" executable from
its own core dump.

Long-running jobs could call checkpoint at various parts in their execution
so that, if the system did crash, they could be restarted from the last
checkpoint.

Checkpoints didn't work real well with jobs that modified permanent (disk
or tape) data since the checkpoint didn't record the data changes.  Thus
it could be dangerous to restart a job from a checkpoint if the job had
actually done a lot of processing and writing to disk/tape after the point
at which the last checkpoint was taken.  Obviously, on an orderly shutdown
where all the running jobs could be paused and THEN checkpointed, it wasn't
a problem.

Checkpointing is real easy to do in a batch environment.  Much more difficult
to do in a terminal/client server environment where you will lose various
connections when the system goes down.

Next topic:  Who wants to talk about 7-track tapes?  Gee, I remember when
we got the 1600 BPI (from 556BPI) units, THAT was some storage.  We
didn't know what to do with ourselves when the 6250 (?) BPI tapes came along.

Nothing beats the sound of those vacuum columns.

greg


p.s.  The CDC used 7-track tapes because it had 6-bit characters.  They
      were the same reel-reel tapes others (IBM, etc) used in 9-track
      drives.
