The BR14 part is too easy.  IEFBR14 clears register 15, and then
does a branch to the contents of register 14 (e.g., "BR @14").

Of course, according to urban legend, the first coding for IEFBR14
had just a single instruction, "BR @15".  Then, the programmer having
realized his mistake when the loop occurred, corrected it to be a
"BR @14".  However, it was only later that it was realized that the
return code was not set to zero.  And, subsequent to this, it was
realized that the copyright statement was missing....

Dave

P.S.  I don't know the significance of 13030 and 19069, but I think I should.
Oh, wait, isn't 13030 the maximum track capacity of a 3330?  And, 19069 is
the track capacity of a 3350.




