Ugh, who else remembers PR1MOS?  Was there anything right about that
operating system?  I remember the machine being kinda sexy (with all
the protection rings, et. al) but OS (which was written in Fortran) was
as strange as they got.

It had two standard prompts:

	"OK, "

		- and -

	"ER! "

You got the second one if the command you just executed returned an error.

I recall that you could change the prompt to anything you liked so long
as it was exactly four characters.  Hmmm, do I smell 32 bits around here?

It had a filesystem tree, like Unix, except that there were multiple
roots and the directory separator was a ">" which is much harder to type,
on most keyboards, than "/".  A path was something like:

	<DISK01>USERS>GREG>MYFILE.F

The only thing worse was the absolutely BEWILDERING array of options to
the compilers and linkers; most having to do with which type of
executable you were making.  16 bit, 32 bit, 32 bit with salami, 32 bit
with some 16 bit and a side of coleslaw, etc.

And you felt the "Fortranness" of the operating system all the time.

The machine came out when everyone and their brother was coming out
with superminicomputers (DEC w/VAX, PR1ME, DG w/Eclipse).  We know who
won that battle...

Whatever happened to the company?  I remember seeing their building just
outside of Boston ten or fifteen years ago.

greg
