: Yes, this is silly. Here are some timings, on a full QCD
: code with staggered quarks, 5 MonteCarlo steps, 100
: molecular dynamics steps... quark mass: 0.01, beta=5.6:

: 133MHz Dell P5                       :   35  Mflops/s
: 150MHz Dell P5 notebook              :   40  Mflops/s
: 200MHz Dell ppro (200MHz, 256k cache):  105 Mflops/s
: 200MHz dual ppro (200, 256)          :  195 Mflops/s

: 333MHz 21164 (Alphastation 500)      :   76 Mflops/s

Ah ... this is really quite interesting. (thinking out loud)

You are obviously not a fool, so are using great chunks of -O7327
-tune ev5 options to tell the compilers what to do. 

If you're working in floating point, you don't get the vast speedup
that multi-precision arithmetic gets from 64bit registers.

The Alphastation has a fair amount of cache (>>256K), so that
won't be a problem.

Does the program have large numbers of byte arrays, which are
supposedly something the Alpha falls down and dies on?

I'm sure, if you post a few inner loops and let the zealots loose
on them, you'll get at least some speedup and some very interesting
info on Alpha optimisation, all for free ... I'd certainly watch
and be amazed.

