Alfred Falk <falk@arc.ab.ca.nospam>:

> Now for a command language that came closer to realizing its
> designers intentions, I would recommend Data General's AOS, AOS/VS
> CLI.  Like a lot of good technology,. you may consider it defunct.

I find that a little surprising. I did a good bit of CLI programming
(for fun) back in college on a Data General, and I thought it was
strnage how the language had several relatively powerful commands
while also lacking in so many basics. There was no control transfer of
any kind; but if you needed to iterate, you could call yourself
recursively, using different command-line parameters to differentiate
from when you were executed the first time. (You could?  You had to!)
The only way to manipulate string values was with the explode
pseudomacro, which would break a value into separate characters, which
you could then pass on your (or some other program's) command line. At
the same time it had sophisticated error-trapping mechanisms (which I
never fully mastered). No do, no while, no for, but it did have some
extremely flexible ways to encode a series of similar commands in a
single line.

While I enjoyed CLI immensely, I would not have guessed that it was
designed that way.

b



