:Stephen Hoff Hoffman asked:
:
:>    What OpenVMS version(s) are involved at each end?
:>    Is breakin evasion active?  (SHOW INTRUSION)
:>    Are any SYSMAN patches installed?
:
:Answers:
:1) local OpenVMS : 5.5-2H4
:   remote OpenVMS: 5.5-2

   Please consider an upgrade, as these releases are very old.

:2) no intrusion

   As the intrusion support was first added in V6.0, it would
   be very interesting if you found intrusion records.  :-)

:3) I don't know whether there are SYSMAN patches installed

   Assuming that the remote username is:
        o not disusered, captive or restricted, or if
          network access is disabled,
           ...If it is, well, fix it.  :-)  Or use a
	   different username for the SYSMAN access.
        o that the password and username are correct,
           ...if not, then use the right one...
        o has not been recently changed,
           ...if it has been changed, then either try
           another (bogus) username and password -- to
           flush the last-username-used cache in SYSMAN
	   -- then retry the original username with the
	   new password.  Or simply log out and back in,
	   and try the SYSMAN command again.
        o A SYSUAF logical name is not in use.
           ...If SYSUAF is redirected via logical name,
           make certain the logical name is defined early
           in the system bootstrap -- SYLOGICALS.COM is
           often the best -- where it will be picked up
           when SYSMAN starts up.  Reboot, or (as a very
	   short-term workaround :-), add the username
	   and password pair to whichever SYSUAF file
	   SYSMAN is using...

    If the above show nothing obvious, then consider pulling over
    and applying a copy of the "VAXMANA02_070" (or later) for
    application on both systems.  This patch applies to OpenVMS
    VAX V5.5-2 through V7.0.  This patch is available at Digital's
    patch area: http://www.service.digital.com/html/patch_public.html

