Excuse me for my obvious pro-Alpha bias but I just want to 
point out a glaring inconsistency in the above comparison.
I'll ignore the obvious effects of using a Msft compiler
(or other non-Digital compilers) since this _only_ amounts
to about a 15% improvement.  The biggest oversight here is
the two chips you mention.  You mention "a couple of years
ago" so I am assuming that the p-90 was pretty much leading
edge technology in mid 1995.  (My memory fails me as to 
what time this chip was first shipping.)  Since you're saying
you used NT on a 166MHz system I'm making the wild guess
that you were using a 21066 based system.  (I could be
wrong but some assumptions need to be made.)  Even if it
were a 21064 based system the argument still holds true that
you were putting low end 1992 technology against high end
1995 technology.  Sounds fair to me.  Fair in the sense that
I can compare a 500 MHz 21164 based system against a 90 Mhz
Pentium based system.  (SPECint ~15 vs ~ 2.9)  Admittedly, up
until the last few weeks you couldn't have bought a cheap Alpha
system that could compete with an equivalently priced Intel
system but that is another story for another time.

>Besides, stirring up a little lively conversation is fun.

Agreed, but make sure you have some shread of "believability" 
in your discussions.  (By the way I'm not claiming that Alphas
beat everything, everywhere, ...  that would be silly.)

- Rob



