In article <32DBD509.25A3@ktb.net>, Mike Boning <boneman@ktb.net> writes:
:Can anyone tell me what class of VAX I have? The hw_name says,
:"MicroVAX 3100-30/40". If you can direct me to the right place, I have
:other Vaxes that I would like to verify by "class". Sometimes I see the
:class represented by numbers and sometimes by "entry", "workgroup",
:"departmental", and "enterprise". I would be grateful for a look at
:DEC's descriptions and a reference chart for Vaxes and Alphas.

  Information on currently available systems is available at Digital's
  web site -- www.digital.com -- in the Systems and Options Catalog,
  and/or the DECdirect catalog.

  The MicroVAX 3100 model 30 (and model 40) systems are low-end VAX
  server systems -- I expect most folks would consider these systems
  as "entry-level" VAX servers.

  The current families of VAX systems are the 3100 series, the 4000
  series, and the 7000 series.  Recent Alpha-based server systems
  include the 400 series, 1000 series, 2000 series, 4000 series,
  and the 8000 series.

  Rather than trying to fit the system into a "class", you'll want
  to obtain a general idea of the target application(s) and target
  loading for the system, and determine which system best fits your
  application requirements and your budget.

  As for the meaning of "class" -- some of the specific system(s) that
  might be in a particular class is certainly arguable -- one could
  think of a system's overall capacity and performance as being
  "small", "medium", "large", or "extra large".

