My opinion on GUI vs VMS:

Windows is bad because it lacks a command line interface and decent
batch files. This applies to the MAC as well.

GUI is good for certain operations, and command line is good for other
stuff. Command line recall saves much more time than a GUI could in many
operations.

GUI is good for desktop stuff (word processing, charts, drawings or any
other stuff that should look good). But GUI is not as good for managing
systems where command-line is more productive to those who know it well.

GUI is more intuitive and easier to learn. Command-line takes some time
to learn and get comfortable. (VMS might be more intuitive than UNIX,
but that just saves you a few hours of training/getting used to).

Personally, to write programs, I am much more comfortable on a VT320
with TPU/EVE than I am on a GUI editor. I find it much easier on the
eyes to be on a amber over black screen than those darn black over white
GUI screens with fancy proportional fonts or letters that are much
smaller than on a good old VT terminal.

For what it is worth, I started to use a MAC before I started to use VT
terminals on VMS.



