In article <5bfhss$1jn9@rs18.hrz.th-darmstadt.de>, leinberger@axp621.gsi.de (Uwe Leinberger GSI Darmstadt) writes:
>you overlook the fact that never ever M$ has brought out anything that 
>was NOT defined by marketroid-hype. NEVER will any good technical argument 
>have any real impact on any M$ product. And THIS is why mission/business critical
>stuff is NOT run on M$-ware like Windoze or NT, but on stable, reliable platforms.
>Yes, I know, there is a lot of hype about NT and it's virtues and about the death 
>of the dinosaurs as well. But look at the real world: NT or the like will only run
>some uncritical apps like a bit of mail, word, excel (shudder...never rely on any 
>excel calculations!) and a terminal emulator, but the CRITICAL stuff, ie REAL WORK 
>is still manily done on dino's like expensive MVS mainframes or VMS clusters or the 
>like.

Do not overlook the wisdom of producing products that the market, for whatever
reason, is willing to pay for.  Look at UNIX.  Most UNIX systems today don't
even run a layered product capable of providing batch and print queue services
inferior to what was in VMS V1.0, and UNIX security lets almost anyone become
root via sendmail bugs and countless programs that let long command lines dance
on the stack, yet UNIX has overtaken VMS' share of the enterprise.  UNIX
clusters don't have block-served disks or a distributed lock manager, but the
add-on Oracle Parallel Server protocol allows Oracle's database engine to take
advantage of clustering.  The operating system is simply no longer the most
important part of the system.  The Oracle and Informix databases and the mail
and groupware services are how the enterprises see their systems, and the
computer people earn their paychecks by doing whatever it takes to keep the
Oracle and Informix databases and the mail and groupware services reliable and
available.

>If there is not a (IMHO impossible) total change in the way a "product" evolves 
>within/out of M$, they will NEVER get any reliability/security/desaster recovery 
>in their systems.

NT V4.0 and SQL Server V6.5 are not even close to enterprise worthy.  As
Digital's marketing destroyed VMS and almost the company by flagrant
incompetence, Microsoft's marketing will keep the heat on the products until
the market buys millions of them.  They needed cluster technology, so they
signed a deal with Digital.  Once they get all the cluster technology they
need, they will do to Digital what they did to IBM over OS/2.  NT V5.0 will
have kerberos and be more scalable, and NT V6.0 or V7.0 or later will
eventually catch up with UNIX, because Microsoft's marketing people will keep
that focus, and the corporation has the time, resources, and motivation to do
it.

>You ignore this and go M$ for your critical stuff at your own risk......

Today, true.  Tomorrow, maybe.  Some day later, UNIX will be the niche player
that VMS already is.  Be prepared.

Tony Scandora, Argonne National Lab, 630-252-7541
scandora@cmt.anl.gov



