In article <1997Jan15.155414@elias.decus.ch>, t_schmid@elias.decus.ch writes:
> That's the old annoying problem: you changed your password on the 
> remote node XYZ at some stage, but the SMISERVER process there still
> remembers the old password.
> 
> Login to XYZ, do a STOP/IDEN=<SMISERVER-pid> there.
> Restart SMISERVER on XYZ with
>    $ @SYS$SYSTEM:STARTUP SMISERVER

That take privilege on XYZ. All you need to do is force SMISERVER to
reread the password. Since it only caches one, make a bogus attempt to
use SYSMAN with another username, then go back to your username.

SYSMAN> SET ENVIRONMENT/NODE=XYZ/USERNAME=some_other_name
SYSMAN> DO SHOW TIME (which will fail unless you know some_other_name's
password)
SYSMAN> SET ENVIRONMENT/NODE=XYZ

SMISERVER now reads your current password and you're all set.

