>Uwe Leinberger GSI Darmstadt wrote:
>> you overlook the fact that never ever M$ has brought out anything that
>> was NOT defined by marketroid-hype. NEVER will any good technical argument
>> have any real impact on any M$ product. And THIS is why mission/business critical
>> stuff is NOT run on M$-ware like Windoze or NT, but on stable, reliable platforms.
>
>It used to be that big jobs just HAD to run on IBM mainframes because
>IBM mainframes were the only big machines capable of handling big loads.
>(hence banks going IBM).
>Even though faster machines came along, IBM shops stuck with expensive
>mainframes because they were brainwashed by IBM into thinking that these
>were puny unreliable toys incapable of handling the loads. I heard that
>said about an ALPHA which was faster than a 150 mips IBM mainframe.
>
>HOWEVER, the IBM brainwashing is not as pervasive as before because even
>magazines such as Datamation acknowledge that PCs and especially NT have
>gained respectability. As a result of this marketing, managers are
>starting to look at alternatives.
>
>
>Because of the archaic and expensive architecture of IBM mainframes,
>you'll find those 7*24 shops will be using different systems more and
>more for new applications. Not only because of costs being lower, but
>because they are forced by the availability of softwrae only on those
>newer "smaller" platforms.
>
>For instance, telephone banking doesn't run on IBM mainframes, it runs
>on smaller boxes, even PCs in some instances (which send transaction to
>the mainframe).
>
>As time progresses, you'll find the mainframe transformed into a
>glorified file/transaction server at which point, it will be repleacable
>by commodity products supporting SQL etc on NT.
>
>While it is fair to say that NT isn't quite ready yet, it is also fair
>to say that because of M$'s marketing, determination and endless
>resources, NT will make serious inroads into what used to be "reliable
>and expensive" systems.
>
>With this in mind, I understand DEC's attitude towards VMS: "keep it
>alive for a while longer and once NT has proven itself, customers will
>migrate on ther own towards a much cheaper platform".

For most of these critical applications, there's a LOT more to be
considered than just speed of processing and cost of the system.
The availability of the system needs to be considered.  An outage
can adversely affect the profitability, or even the survivability
of a company.  Mainframes, in my experience, have a much higher
reliability than do workstations and LANs.  This may be partially due
to more reliable hardware, or error tolerant designs, or it may be due
to more reliable software (operating systems and applications), or a
variety of other reasons.

Another consideration is data backup and archival.  While PC/LAN systems
are much better than they used to be, I don't think there's any doubt
that mainframes are still superior in this area.

Yet another aspect of this is data security.  Again, while PC/LAN systems
are improving, they have a LONG way to go before they rival the security
of the traditional mainframes.

Finally, data integrity is an issue.  How many mainframe viruses have
you counted?  How does this compare to PC virsus?  Which system would
you want to have keeping track of your savings?  (I'm not saying that
mainframe virsuses are impossible, just that there are lots fewer of them
in existance than there are workstation viruses.).

PC/LAN systems have their use, and workstations are performing some of
the tasks formerly run on mainframes.  However, I hope that everyone who
has critical data or applications has considered these points.

Dave

P.S. Yes, I work for IBM, but these opinions are strictly my own.




