pdwalker@ wrote:

>Then it shows your youth.

>The first real computer that I was introduced to was a CDC-Cyber 6600.  
>Apparently they were considered to be a nice machine.  Unfortunately they 
>were also very hard to use.  Actually, the commands on the system made 
>any unix command user friendly and naturally intutitive.

Small quibble, was not the machine just called the 6600.  The "Cyber" thing came
a bit later.  I am not sure of this, but if there is some old Control Data types
out there, I am sure we will get the right answer.

>The computer's OS was called NOS, which the system programmers called 
>"No Operating System".  They were only half-joking.

In defense of the 6600, it was at a time when it was not at all clear what an OS
should do and what an "application" should do.  NOS was very sparce.  As I
remember every command just executed a program.  Even the definition of data
executed something.

The machine was designed for number crunching, which it did quite well.  My
guess is that the folks from the frozen north figured that the services of the
OS were modest and that most of the time the beast would be doing math.  Just
supply it with the bare bones, and let 'er rip.

Of course, computing went the other way with, heaven forbid, OS/360 trying to be
all things to all machines.  We now think of the OS as king.  We look for kings
to be powerful.
