>>>
>>>You are correct.  The 6600 was just a 6600.  The "Cyber" moniker
>>>was introduced with machines like the Cyber 170 line which were 70s
>>>vintage machines.

>	The 6600 consisted of a single heavy-duty (high performance)
>	number cruncher w/ 60bit words and 12 I/O service processors
>	using (IIRC) 15bit words.

There's a picture of the architecture on my home page at:
	http://gtravis.ucs.indiana.edu/Engines/Compute/index.html

The "CPU" portion of the machine consisted of several functional units
(ADD/MULTIPLY/BOOLEAN/JUMP/...) which could execute their instructions
in parallel if there were no register conflicts.

Instructions were 15/30/60 bit so you could have up to 4 instructions
per word.  Odd combos (30 bit instruction followed by a 15 and then another
30) were handled by padding words with NOPs (15 bit instruction).
Supergeek time was working on your COMPASS (assy language) code all
night to minimize the number of NOPs generated and maximize the overlap
of the functional units.

The CPU at first had absolutely no I/O instructions of any kind.  All IO was
handled by 12 bit (not 15 bit) peripheral processors (PPUs) which themselves
were full computers.  The PPUs could read/write central memory and had
channels attached to them.  A CPU program requested IO by writing a
request in a certain memory location.  A special PPU program (CIO) would
read that address (each "process" had its own address - kind of a memory
mailbox) after a while and schedule a free PPU to process it.  Since it
could sometimes take a while between when a CPU program wrote a request and
when CIO noticed it, an option was introduced which added the "XJ"
instruction.  XJ stood for "eXchange Jump".  It, in effect, interrupted
CIO and woke it up immediately.  So a CPU program would write its
request and then execute an XJ instruction on machines equipped with the
option.

Without XJ, the user-level CPU program had to go into a loop waiting for the
request to be picked up and for CIO to schedule another CPU program
to run while it worked on the first's I/O request.  This wasted a lot
of CPU cycles.

The machine had 18 bit addressing, allowing for a maximum of 256K of
60 bit words which was roughly equivalent to 2 megabytes of today's
storage.  Smallest addressable unit (by the CPU) was a full 60 bit
word so a lot of shifting and masking went on to extract characters.  The
character set was 6 and 12 bit; started out 6 but went to 12 when they
needed more characters; ended up wasting a lot of bits in an 8-bit world.

The CPU had a small 15 word (I think) "cache" which consisted of transistor
memory.  It was really just a window on the next 15 instructions.  If you
could get a loop to fit within the window, it ran blazingly fast.  It did
have real interesting implications for self-modifying code though.  One
COMPASS assembly program that I had wrote had a section which "jumped" to
an appropriate display driver (which was determined at system startup).

Since 6600 had no JUMP TO <ADDRESS IN REGISTER> instruction, I constructed
a longjump instruction at runtime and then placed the construction in the
next instruction address (this was before split instruction/data spaces or
read-only text segments).  The program ran just fine on our "modern" 1970s
Cyber 172 (which had no instruction cache and was significantly slower than
the 1964 6600) but failed on the 6600.  I beat my head against the wall until
I realized that that portion was fitting in the instruction cache which
was being filled by the modified word before I got around to modifying it.
Sticking a bunch of NOPs between where I wrote the instruction and where it
was executed solved the problem!

PPUs had their memory set up as 12 bit words.

The entire machine was architected around its main memory, as opposed to
architected around the CPU as is common today.  Main memory consisted of
four independent banks of 60 bit words.  The banks were interleaved in
the address range.  Address 0 went to bank 0, address 1 to bank 1, address
2 to bank 2, address 3 to bank 3, and address 4 to bank 0 again.

As I recall, it was nominally good for about 1 million instructions per
second.  That doesn't sound too impressive until you factor in the fact that
each instruction could (for example) multiply two 60-bit numbers and that,
if you coded right and didn't have too many memory bank conflicts,
you could get four functional units busy, each at 1 million instructions/sec.

>	Cooling was via freon refrigerant, the modules were pretty simple.

>	I heard cute stories about it too.

I still have a 4K main memory module from ours.  Imagine a cube about
4" on a side and weighing about 10LBS.

>>Umm, exactly which "modern IBM operating system" are you referring to?
