In article <5bl0on$qku@uni.library.ucla.edu> tmark@psych.ucla.edu writes:
> I am thinking about buying - partly to play with and partly to experiment
> with as a web-server - a VX40A-F2.  These can be had for very little,
> it seems.  I assume this is because they are more or less obsolete.

Exactly.

> I am having difficulty finding information on these machines, however.
> DEC's home page turned up no mention of them (that I could find). 

http://www.brouhaha.com/~eric/computers/udb.html

> - what is TGA video ?

TGA is a DEC-proprietary video controller.  In the Multia it does 8 bpp.

> Can these things be plugged into a standard VGA monitor ?

Yes, it has the ubiquitous 15 pin subminiature D connector.
Avoid fixed-frequency monitors though.

> - how much RAM am I going to need to run Linux and an httpd ? 

The Multia normally shipped with 24 MB.  I'd recommend at least 32 MB.
I upgraded mine from 24 MB to 40 MB and there was quite an improvement in
performance.

> - does Apache compile under Linux on these things ?

Yes.  Red Hat Linux/AXp comes with Apache.

> - how much would one pay for one of these machines with no RAM, monitor,
> or video ?

They go for around $400 with 24 MB of RAM and an internal 2.5 inch 340 MB
disk (but no monitor or keyboard).

> - any caveats ?

Make sure you get a unit that has an external SCSI connector.  Even if you
have the internal drive, it is too small and too slow.

Also, make sure the unit really is an -F2 instead of an -F3.  The -F3 had
a 3.5 inch internal disk, which didn't leave room for a PCI card.  Even
if you take the disk out, the riser card doesn't have the PCI connector.

Cheers,
Eric
