	UNIVAC 1100 series, too, which was a "relatively sane" OS for
	the time period.

>Umm, exactly which "modern IBM operating system" are you referring to?
>MVS?  DOS/VSE?  VM-CMS?  AIX?  OS/2?  PC-DOS?

	We're talking more like OS/MFT, OS/MVT, MVS/TSO, etc.  DOS/VS
	wasn't a prizewinner, either.  The IBM OSs for such systems
	weren't optimized for multiple users (though VM/CMS had "virtual
	minidisks" allocated to a user) since most of the work was done
	via batch jobs (launched either from a card deck or invokded by
	a console command).

	It was a *very* different time from anything Unix or DOS users
	are familiar with.  In some ways, IMHO, OS|2's internal
	architecture looks more like the mainframe systems than I would
	otherwise have believed possible.

	Yes, I'm an *old* curmudgeon;  I'm so old that when *I* break
	wind, dust comes out...					:-) :-)

