Rich Lafferty <lafferty@cs.mcgill.ca> writes:

>Nothing else? Either you are naive, sir, or ignorant. I challenge you to prove
>to me that ARPAnet and later the Internet proper chose Unix-based systems
>because of low cost and a bunch of university hackers.

No, the system of choice for the ARPANET was not Unix, nor was it VMS.

It was Tops-20 (or its predecessor TENEX) running on a PDP-10 ("DECSYSTEM-20").
But that was priced by DEC, so CS departments which could only afford 11/780's
with Unix had to roll their own, and that's why you see any Unix here at all.

TCP/IP for VMS was done by guys who had worked on -10's before moving on, again
a matter of rolling their own.

Desktop implementations lose the way Unix does, so there you are.
