I remember working at a shop running DOS/VSE without VSAM. Picture a
system person standing up and shoutin "OK, who used tracks XXX to YYY on
pack ABC.... I just lost my big important file" ..... (With someone else
in the area getting an instant big blush and hiding under their desk).

Yep, to keep stuff on a disk, you had to request to the system admins
actual space on a pack for each big data file and they had to keep track
of where and what was on their disks !!!!!

For programmers, VM was pretty nice though, and has a smarter file
allocation scheme, but it was not possible to access those files from
batch jobs.

They eventually installed a file management (I beleive it came with
VSAM) over those packs and this finally managed disk allocations.

I still remember how amazed I was that programmers had to know the
physical location of their files on disk !

And I remember one guy telling me how smart VMS was because when you
submitted a job, instead of spooling the whole file (to print or
execute), it only sent a pointer to the file !!!! This was back in 82-83
timeframe.



