Subject: I got the 3D sound to work on my Starion 911...
Date: 1997/01/09
Message-ID: <32D4AE72.1250@nwlink.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 208667964
content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: Northwest Link
mime-version: 1.0
reply-to: ronk@nwlink.com
newsgroups: comp.sys.ibm.pc.soundcard.tech,comp.sys.dec
x-mailer: Mozilla 3.0Gold (Win95; U)


Despite the fact that the box that my Starion 911 came in said it had 3D
sound, I was never able to get it to work and the DEC tech support told
me the 911 doesn't have 3D sound.

I d/l the ESS Windows95 driver packet from their web site
(www.esstech.com).  As the readme file says, I modified the inf file to
install for the ES938 hardware.  In fact, here's what it says:

"If your hardware supports 3-D and Tone control, i.e. includes ES938
hardware, the Windows95 drivers need to have the 3-D/Tone support
enabled.  To do so, the OEMSETUP.INF (of ESS Windows95 drivers
diskette) needs to be modified prior to installing the Windows95
drivers.  The OEMSETUP.INF contains a section named [ES938.AddReg]
which allows an OEM to enable the installation of the controls
for ES938."

I removed the appropriate semicolons to initiate support.  There were
some other features to turn on but I don't know what they do yet.  I
then installed the ES1688 drivers (after removing the old) and ran the
3D sound program I've kept around but has never made a difference in
sound before... walla, I got 3D sound (or at least something that sounds
like 3D sound).

There was also the driver for ES1688 PnP.  There's a jumper on the sound
card where I can enable PnP, but what's the advantage of that?  Doesn't
everyone know that soundcards use 220/388/330/5/1 settings so how could
there be a conflict in W95?  It seems to me that PnP could easily screw
up DOS games if I ran them from W95.  Does anyone know the advantages of
using the PnP driver (and jumper)?

A friend emailed me the AudioDrive32 program that came with when
you purchase the ESS card.  It works just like W95 CD player, except it
supports 3D and Tone control.  When I used the AD32 program, I couldn't
operate the 3D and tone control functions.  Today, I had to run the 3D
program again to turn on 3D sound, so I don't know when it turns off.

Anyways, I just wanted to share what I found and wondered if anyone else
had something to add.

ronk@nwlink.com



