Peter Mayne (Peter.Mayne@cao.mts.dec.com) wrote:
: Jason Taylor wrote:
: > Clue 4: Only super performance freaks would want to have to learn how
: >   to use two totally different computer systems instead of just one.
: > 
: > & Clue 4 ----> Purchased office computers will be of the same
: >       software/operating system as the popular home PCs (though
: >       possibly at a worse price/value).]
: > 
: >         And that, my friends, is why more Alphas are not purchased
: > today.

: Minor nit: you can run Windows NT with MSOffice on your office x86
: computer, and take the MSOffice kit home and install it on your Windows
: NT Alpha home computer. Same software, same operating system.

Neat.  FX!32 (and running x86 apps on an alpha) sounds cool.  But
none of that matters if DEC doesn't WANT and make a realistic effort
to sell to the home market.  Face it, NT isn't supposed to be for home
use. Consequently, it really isn't that popular (and won't be).  I
mean, there are probably dozens of half-baked emulators out there that
run anything on anything if you pay enough and try hard enough.  But until
I have gone to CompUSA to buy a DEC that was sitting on the shelf with
software at good price/values points (whole systems for $1000 or less)
for use in my HOME, DEC will not have a home presence and I would not
purchase their computers to replace the x86 one sitting on my OFFICE
desk now.

If you plot the UNIX to x86 market share ratio as a function of time,
I think you would find a curve that is going downhill.  Yes, there is
a performance niche market that doesn't really care about whether or
not the most popular word processor of the system (for UNIX, vi or
emacs) has point-and-click home-system ease of use, but the rest of us
are going to get an x86 like our home system.  Moreover, most of us
will just run illegal copies of some of our office software at home.

DEC, SUN, and other UNIX machine makers are forgetting one of the 10
anti-commandments of business: know what you are selling.  (Which a
good student of business will know can put you at 100% market share
but in a dead market; a successful company needs to know what the
consumer is really buying, which in this case is normally a box that
works like the one at home.)

: Your deduction from clue 4 doesn't imply that that office/home
hardware : will be the same.  : -- : PJDM Digital.

True.  However, we can continue from the original logic slightly:
since economies of scale requires high volumes, and prices must be
good for popularity in either market, and price/performance is hit
upon emulation, the home and office hardware will ALSO be the same at the
processor level.
