I'd check out linux, not sure of the exact cu-seeme functionality
but I've used linux as a client on the mbone with no problem, mrouted
is available and I THINK thats all you need.

> 1) I'll be running UNIX; is there a reason to pay the extra money for
> Digital UNIX, or should I go with Linux?  I am not an experienced system
> administrator, but neither am I a novice.  I am not particularly familiar
> with either DU or Linux, but assume that the former has better
> documentation and support (perhaps I am wrong).

I'd go with linux if it has the functionality that you need.  There
are many newsgroups, mainling lists, and users.  Lotsa people
in highschool/college are using linux.  I.e. theres lotsa linux
experience you can tap into without a support contract.

> 2)  Should I spend the extra money on a SCSI system, or should I get one
> with EIDE drives?  I've heard that SCSI outperforms EIDE significantly,
> but have no direct experience with this.

The $5k Alpha Personal Worksation includes 64 mb ram, 2.1 gb scsi,
8*cdrom, ethernet, 433 Mhz cpu, matrox video (Just reading from
the web page).  Probably not a monitor but not sure.

This is a bit more then some of the 3rd party clones but it's
a significantly redesigned machine.   Faster memory system, lower latency,
ultra wide scsi+EIDE+ethernet on board.

> 3)  How fast should the processor be?  I'm tempted to say "as fast as I
> can afford," but I'm not going to be doing 3-D modelling or CAD/CAM on
> this machine, so perhaps would be better off getting a 200-300 MHz
> processor and a larger disk.

For your pusposes I'd get a 433 Mhz cpu, should be plenty fast, the
500 Mhz bundles seem to include a 3-d graphics controller which will
only be worth it if you use it.  Not sure if 3rd party's are going
to start selling the sdram based alpha motherboards in the near
future or not.

> 4)  Where should I buy the machine?  I know I can buy it from DEC, but I
> have heard that there are cheaper sources available.  Are they just as
> good?

None of the current alpha clones are sdram based.  The Personal alpha
workstation is faster at floating point with NO cache then the pc164
with 1 mb cache from teh clones. (at 500 Mhz).

I'd rather have a highspeed/low latency memory system then a big cache
and slow memory.

