[ John Saunders ]

>>  The market has decided that OpenVMS and Digital UNIX are not desktop OSs.
>>  That is the market these NT-only boxes are aimed at. DEC cannot sell what
>>  customers will not buy, and they'd better not waste their money developing
>>  what customers won't buy.

Sounds like a case of self-fulfilling prophecy:  If a desktop system running
DUnix costs substantially more than the exact same hardware running NT, this
is bound to make NT look more attractive to the bean-counters and the upper
level managers, who often have to buy systems by the zillions, and for whom
the cost difference is therefore a non-trivial issue.

It's true that "DEC cannot sell what customers will not buy", but perhaps if
there weren't a cost penalty for choosing UNIX over NT, people might actually
buy more of the former.

There *are* people who do want UNIX on their desktop systems, and Digital has
made a conscious decision not to serve the needs of those customers, letting
other vendors (and the linux crowd) have that market niche.

We have a moderate amount of DEC hardware (six single-CPU machines, and a pair
of four-CPU AlphaServers), and would like to continue buying systems running
DUnix (I do enjoy linux, but DUnix has things like strong compiler technology
going for it), but it looks as if we're unlikely to be buying much of anything
else from Digital anytime soon, unless their strategy changes.

