On 3 Jan 1997 13:18:25 -0500, in alt.folklore.computers,
rdd@access2.digex.net (R. D. Davis) wrote:

>I suppose that next, someone will be
>claiming that Windows '95 is nicer to work with than X Windows.

Actually, I find the most annoying part of the Windows user interface
is that it doesn't differentiate between 'in focus' and 'foreground'.
There's a utility available from Microsoft in their 'Power Toys'
package which makes 95's windows work a lot more like X' windows.

Getting back to the original thread: does anyone consider VMS to be
even vaguely user-friendly?

Doesn't everyone buy that it's damned hard to balance the stability
and security of an OS like VMS with concerns like user-friendliness,
browsing, and similarity to existing Windows APIs?!?

Best of all, the traditional pool of Windows applications programmers
don't have a clue what it's like to write a server, and NT makes no
allowances for this.  Example: Sybase SQL/Anywhere (formerly Watcom
SQL Server), NT edition, runs as an application and not as a service.
This means that it won't run unless a user has logged on to the NT
_console_, which is Real Good for coming up after long power failures.

Microsoft wants the applications server market and people think that
NT is the answer because of all of the Windows apps out there that
will be ported... but the fact remains that Microsoft still doesn't
understand that you can't build an applications server which treats
non-console as a real pain-in-the-ass special case.

