Dennis Moran wrote:
> 
> Erik Evensen <erik@avalon.harvard.edu> wrote in article
> <to3ewc6t4u.fsf@avalon.harvard.edu>...
> > Hi,
> >
> > I'm considering building a Pentium Pro or Alpha (21064 - don't think I
> > can afford a 21164 unless I can get mboard/CPU/cache for < $2000)
> > machine to run some pretty big floating point intensive simulations
> > (ca. 180 meg). I'm planning on putting 256 meg in the machine. My
> > question is how does a PPro perform with large programs -- is the 256K
> > cache a big problem (would the 512K PPro make more sense). How does
> > memory bandwidth compare between PPro and Alpha motherboards/chipsets?
> >
> > Thanks for any input. . .
> >
> > --erik
> >
> >
> 
> The Alpha will really kick the Pentium Pro's ass in what you are
> describing. You can get a 433MHz Alpha system for $4000 or so dollars. The
> memory bandwidth is much faster on the Alpha system.

This does not automatically follow. As a developer of extremely floating
point intensive software, I (and many in the scientific community) have
been extremely disappointed with the performance of the alpha. Sure,
they
perform well on the benchmarks, but when it comes to the [number]
crunch,
I would go with a ppro (better value). Take a particular simulation I am 
working on in quantum chromodynamics: a dual ppro (200MHz) performs
almost 
as well as a 16 node T3D!!! Talking about hype, the alpha has been hyped
to
hell and back. So don't always believe the hype. My dell notebook is
TWICE as
fast as a node on the Cray T3D, with its alpha CPU and huge L2 cache,
and
massive price tag. The advantage of the ppro lies in the large fast L2
cache,
and it makes a big difference in serious scientific computing.



