Geoffrey Welsh wrote:
> allowances for this.  Example: Sybase SQL/Anywhere (formerly Watcom
> SQL Server), NT edition, runs as an application and not as a service.
> This means that it won't run unless a user has logged on to the NT
> _console_, which is Real Good for coming up after long power failures.

I am not 100% sure this is still the case, but ST400, a serious SWIFT
software running on VMS, transfering billions of dollars a day, would
hang all terminals if the terminal on which the software was started
(and which displayed ST400 console messages) would be controlled-off
(eg: attached printer running out of paper) and there was no way to
redirect the output to a file as the software did logical name
translation and trunctated everything after a ":" (as well as making
liberal use of SET TERM/INQUIRE).

Poorly designed applications exist on all platforms. Initially, you
should expect DOS apps ported to NT to be inferior. But it is my opinion
that it will not take much time before seriously written appls will
appear on NT and each new release of software will move more and more
towards serious systems and further and further from toy-DOS
architectures.



