[...]
> Getting back to the original thread: does anyone consider VMS to be
> even vaguely user-friendly?

        I do.  But  then,  I  could  be  acused  of  being  a crufty old
    geezer...  :-)

> Doesn't everyone buy that it's damned hard to balance the stability
> and security of an OS like VMS with concerns like user-friendliness,
> browsing, and similarity to existing Windows APIs?!?

        I think they're independent  issues  (stability and security vs.
    GUIs and their APIs).  It's a question of designing in the security,
    from  the  beginning,  in the base O/S, and in asking  the  question
    whether a particular application  may  have  security  implications.
    Similarly,  stability  depends  on  putting  a  _value_ on correctly
    engineering code and _testing_  it,  extensively, before it ships to
    customers.   I  don't think you'll find either of these concepts  in
    the wintel market,  either  from  M$  or  from  the  other  software
    vendors.

        FWIW, I used Windows 3.0 for about  3 or 4 years and _never_ did
    like  it  very much.  I find DECwindows/Motif _much_ nicer,  largely
    because of the ability to separate focus from  "topness",  but  also
    because  it  tends to fit my style better.  I _like_ a command line.
    There, I've said it!  But really,  it's  far,  far quicker for me to
    type  in  a  command telling the computer what to do than it  is  to
    click on icons, buttons and menu choices while the computer tells me
    what I'm allowed to do at each point.  BTW, I keep about 15  DECterm
    around  all  the time, as well as typical useful X-applications like
    DECmail and Bookreader.

        This is all to say that every person has their own definition of
    "user-friendly".  Personally, I don't find Windows user friendly, it
    gets in my way  mostly.   But  I  wouldn't  force  a  secretary or a
    manager  to  sit  in  front of a VT200 and use TeX  for  their  word
    processing...  :-}

[...other good points deleted...]

