henley wrote:
> 
> Dennis Moran wrote:
> >
> > Erik Evensen <erik@avalon.harvard.edu> wrote in article
> > <to3ewc6t4u.fsf@avalon.harvard.edu>...
> > > Hi,
> > >
> > > I'm considering building a Pentium Pro or Alpha (21064 - don't think I
> > > can afford a 21164 unless I can get mboard/CPU/cache for < $2000)
> > > machine to run some pretty big floating point intensive simulations
> > > (ca. 180 meg). I'm planning on putting 256 meg in the machine. My
> > > question is how does a PPro perform with large programs -- is the 256K
> > > cache a big problem (would the 512K PPro make more sense). How does
> > > memory bandwidth compare between PPro and Alpha motherboards/chipsets?
> > >
> > > Thanks for any input. . .
> > >
> > > --erik
> > >
> > >
> >
> > The Alpha will really kick the Pentium Pro's ass in what you are
> > describing. You can get a 433MHz Alpha system for $4000 or so dollars. The
> > memory bandwidth is much faster on the Alpha system.
> 
> This does not automatically follow. As a developer of extremely floating
> point intensive software, I (and many in the scientific community) have
> been extremely disappointed with the performance of the alpha. Sure,
> they
> perform well on the benchmarks, but when it comes to the [number]
> crunch,
> I would go with a ppro (better value). Take a particular simulation I am
> working on in quantum chromodynamics: a dual ppro (200MHz) performs
> almost
> as well as a 16 node T3D!!!

Then quess what ?  Your code doesn't parallelize well and you're
not taking advantage of the architecture of the machine.  Multiple
node machines are designed with some very specific tasks in mind,
if your code doesn't happen to fit into that niche, then it's going
to run slow.  The same goes for running non-vectorized code on
the vector machines (I've heard plenty of complaints about how
'slow' some of the Crays are).

 Talking about hype, the alpha has been hyped
> to
> hell and back. So don't always believe the hype. My dell notebook is
> TWICE as
> fast as a node on the Cray T3D, with its alpha CPU and huge L2 cache,
> and
> massive price tag. The advantage of the ppro lies in the large fast L2
> cache,
> and it makes a big difference in serious scientific computing.

To make comments like this you need to know what you're talking about.  
In addition, you also need to less us know that you know what you're talking
about.  In this case, you fail to tell us which variant of the Alpha 
processor is in the T3D.  I can guarrentee you, the T3D is not filled with
the 21164 that everyone's talking about.  For under $5,000 you can get
a complete 500mhz machine that crushes the PPros in floating poing calculations.
That's hardly the 'massive' price tag that you suggest.

Now I've said this many times before.  We run highly floating point
intensive code with little I/O.  We write all our own code in Fortran.
Now, our code on one of the new 433mhz (21164 chip) Alphas runs 
2.6 - 3.0 times as on our 180mhz PPro.  I'll bet you'd see similar 
results if you did the same.
  
I've talked to other people on the net with dual PPro machines.  They
say that for floating point work, their PPros are about as fast as
the 366 mhz Alphas (they have both machines).

Show us some facts about why you think the Alpha is over hyped and
we can talk.  Not just 'my notebook is faster than a supercomputer !',
but some real numbers.  Run your code on the machines and let's see
the results.  Make sure to tell us what Alpha processor you're running
on, though, so we can compare apples with apples.

Jeff



