Lennart Regebro (regebro@stacken.kth.se) wrote:
> crs0794@inforamp.net|no-spam (Geoffrey Welsh) wrote:
>> Getting back to the original thread: does anyone consider VMS to be
>> even vaguely user-friendly?
>
> I remember it as being hugely more user-firendly than Unix. It has
> logical command names, autoexpansion of commands, and better on-line
> help. It feels like a product, not like a patch together of
> fifty-eleven hackers.
>

  The greatest shock for me while starting to learn unix (after working as
  VMS system manager) was that half of unix's utilities were named after
  their writer's pets, and the other half had unreasonably short (not
  to mention the options).

  Combine that with those damn little differences between unix versions
  (e.g. AIX's mail has the -s option, HP's doesnt), and using unix turns
  out to be hard and confusing.

  Yes, I know the claim that I could change command names using aliases,
  but it doesnt help reading other people's scripts, aliases arent
  secure for writing scripts, and I cant do a man command on my aliases,
  nor use them to look for/in manuals.

  Add to that a cryptic man (sure I can read the sources, but I dont want
  to, and dont have the time) [which is inferior by all means when compared
  to VMS's HELP] and the overabundance of utilities [= no programmer in
  the room works with the same utility and utility version, nobody bothers
  to completely debug and integrate all utilities, etc] and I can tell you
  why BSD's logo is a devil.

  I find VMS much more user friendly, not mentioning more feature rich then
  UNIX - distributed lock management, real file system [with indexed files.
  No - I dont want to write my own library for indexed files, and yes - I
  want to access indexed files from a CP], real time scheduling & security
  which arent different from one unix to another [unix lists portability
  as a plus, doesnt it ? well - not if you need real time or security
  support], etc.

 Uri Raz.



