In article <5bb892$19v@news.inforamp.net>, crs0794@inforamp.net|no-spam (Geoffrey Welsh) writes:
>On 3 Jan 1997 13:18:25 -0500, in alt.folklore.computers,
>rdd@access2.digex.net (R. D. Davis) wrote:
>
>>I suppose that next, someone will be
>>claiming that Windows '95 is nicer to work with than X Windows.
>
>Best of all, the traditional pool of Windows applications programmers
>don't have a clue what it's like to write a server, and NT makes no
>allowances for this.  Example: Sybase SQL/Anywhere (formerly Watcom
>SQL Server), NT edition, runs as an application and not as a service.
>This means that it won't run unless a user has logged on to the NT
>_console_, which is Real Good for coming up after long power failures.

One can hardly blame an operating system for being poorly used.  Is SQL
Anywhere an application because that's how its authors wrote it or because
NT can't support it as a service?  How many UNIX programs are built out of 
an incomprehensible shell script of grep, awk, and worse commands?  Does 
that mean you can't write a good UNIX server?

Is NT V4.0 ready for the big time?  Not even close, but Microsoft has the 
determination and the resources to do whatever it takes, no matter how long.
It took them a decade to do it, but they finally came up with a usable 
Windows interface.  Only a year after the introduction of the still inferior
Windows 95, Apple announced the death of the Mac as we know it.  Once the Web
resoundingly trounced the Microsoft Network, Microsoft flooded the market with
an inferior but free browser, which gave them a position until they finally
delivered V3, which works and is still free.  Is it really free?  Of course
not.  Whether you use it or not, you pay for it when you buy Windows.  After
failing in their quest to buy Intuit, they gave away enough copies of Money 95
to get critical mass, and the now well-reviewed Money 97 will be featured on
Oprah tomorrow.  Java came into the world as a run-anywhere system, but
Microsoft quickly figured out how to insert Windows dependencies into it.

For $1500?/year, and less for smaller bundles, you can get all of the CDs and
licenses to run all Microsoft operating systems, database and other servers,
and development tools on your Intel or Alpha computer.  Even if you download
Linux for free, what does it cost you to support software and database
development tools for UNIX or VMS?  What are the odds that UNIX and VMS
developers might start writing serious applications for Windows some day?

Ridicule their technology if you like, but ignore Microsoft at your peril.

Tony Scandora, Argonne National Lab, 630-252-7541
scandora@cmt.anl.gov



