Dave Cherkus <cherkus@unimaster.com> wrote in article
<5alpem$nl3$1@news.destek.net>...
> Terry C. Shannon (shannon@world.std.com) wrote:
> : I sincerely doubt that we'll see DEC retreat into the high-end
workstation 
> : business. Note the price/performance differential between AlphaStations

> : (way pricey) and Alpha-based Personal Workstations (way affordable).
> 
> But aren't these personal workstations NT only?
> 
> This whole trend towards NT-only computers is incredibly daft.
> They've already spent the money to develop the software, so why
> charge a premium above NT to use it?  Gee, SCO gives away its
> OS free if you are a non-commercial single user.  
> 

Dave,

They haven't "already spent the money to develop the software". For each
new machine, there's still a substantial additional investment in the
hardware-specific parts of the operating system. There are also new
peripherals which need to be supported, both add-on and on-board devices.
There is also the additional cost of testing the machines with the
operating system, and the cost of releasing a version of the OS that
supports the new hardware.

Perhaps in the case of these NT-only boxes, DEC has decided that these
costs outweigh the profit from selling these boxes running OpenVMS or
Digital UNIX.

> The only way one can make any sense of this is to come to the
> conclusion DEC wants to get out of the OVMS and UNIX business.

The market has decided that OpenVMS and Digital UNIX are not desktop OSs.
That is the market these NT-only boxes are aimed at. DEC cannot sell what
customers will not buy, and they'd better not waste their money developing
what customers won't buy.

> To me,
> that's even more daft - when they become an NT-only vendor they loose
> any differentiation between them and the zillion other people that can
> provide NT boxes.

They will not become NT-only in the markets which will buy OpenVMS or
Digital UNIX.

Now, if people stop buying OpenVMS and Digital UNIX for high-end servers,
that will be a different story.

-- 
