>I need help diagnosing the following problem: I just replaced my system drive
>on a DEC 3000 300X, After installing OSF 3.2 or OSF 3.0B onto the new drive
>the sytem starts up but shows fragmentation on the root partitions. The
>fragmentation levels are .1% on rrz3a and .3% on rrz3f where I place
>/usr. Sometimes the system still comes up but after I start setting up the
>machine and do a restart (shutdown -r now), the /sbin/rc3.d scripts are 
>corrupted and dump cores when they run.                                                                                            
>I have this problem when I install either operating system and also
>when I install the system to a different DEC hard drive on this system.
>DEC tech support believes it may be the SCSI controller on the system.
>While I continue my testing and tinkering with the system, I would 
>appreciate any pointers on what else might be causing this problem.

DEC may be right.  I just had a similar problem with a 3000 when I
installed a new boot disk.  I kept receiving SCSI errors (very
intermitently (it could run for almost half a day before an error
would crash the system)).  The problem was too many external cabinets
(max of two on a 3000) and the SCSI chaine was too long.  I put in the
smallest DEC cables I could find.  Got the system up and rationalized
the number of disks and cabinets so now it has three disks and one
external cabinet down from six disks and three external cabinets.
Since the rationalisation was done there have been no problems.  One
way to test the scsi bus is in the boot monitor, enter test scsi at
the monitor prompt. eg

>>> test scsi

This sometimes stated that there was a scsi bus fault, as I said the
error was very intermitent.

Greg





