alfred@hw.stratus.com (Alfred von Campe) writes:

> 
> Michael Hoffmann (Michael.Hoffmann@darmstadt.netsurf.de) wrote:
> : Is there any possibility to connect an german LK401-Keyboard (from
> : Digital) to a PC ?
> 
> No, this can't be done.  They use physically and electrically different
> connectors (not to mention a different protocol).  However, an LK411 or
> and LK450 or an LK461 might work, as they use a standard PC connector
> and speak the PC keyboard protocol.  But not all the keys will work
> correctly unless you have a driver for it.
> 
> -- 
> Alfred
> 

Oh boy, ever looked at kernel sources ? (cd /usr/src/linux/drivers/char;
grep LK450 keyb*.c) Ever done (cd /usr/lib/kbd/keytables; ls *lk450*) ?

I patched linux kernel years ago to support this keyboard (must have been
some 0.9x kernel version, don't remember) and gave the patches to Linus
and he included it in the standard kernel. I also made a  sg-latin1-lk450.map
and gave it to the maintainers of the keytables and it is still included
in the keytable package. Since then many changes have been made to the
kernel and the keytable package and I can't verify wether support for the
LK450 is still working because I don't have access to such a keyboard anymore,
but I assume it still works fine.

As you can see from the filename (sg-latin1-lk450.map) I was using a swiss-
german version of that keyboard and it should be fairly easy to create a
de-latin1-lk450.map by looking at the diffs between sg-latin1-lk450.map and
sg-latin1-.map. Obviously nobody else ever needed a keymap for
another national version of that keyboard (or did not bother to give it to
the maintainers of the keytable package), so that's why there is only a
keymap for swiss-german.

have fun

