John Saunders (jws@jws.ultranet.com) wrote:

: Dave,

: They haven't "already spent the money to develop the software". For each
: new machine, there's still a substantial additional investment in the
: hardware-specific parts of the operating system. There are also new
: peripherals which need to be supported, both add-on and on-board devices.
: There is also the additional cost of testing the machines with the
: operating system, and the cost of releasing a version of the OS that
: supports the new hardware.

Yes, that's a major problem DEC has had all along - too many
same-but-different computers.  I've worked with other vendors who had
just as full a product line but made the hardware look the same to the
software.  They had one major design, and just tweaked the size of the
box, the number of IO slots, the clock speed and the cache size to come
up with several different price-points without having to do most of
what you cite above.  These days I beleive they now have three
different designs (small uniprocessor, small smp, enterprise smp)
instead of just one, but far less than DEC.

: Perhaps in the case of these NT-only boxes, DEC has decided that these
: costs outweigh the profit from selling these boxes running OpenVMS or
: Digital UNIX.

I suppose, but it shouldn't be that way.  You make it sound like they
throw out all the work they've ever done in the past and start from
scratch each time a new box shows up.

: > The only way one can make any sense of this is to come to the
: > conclusion DEC wants to get out of the OVMS and UNIX business.

: The market has decided that OpenVMS and Digital UNIX are not desktop OSs.
: That is the market these NT-only boxes are aimed at. DEC cannot sell what
: customers will not buy, and they'd better not waste their money developing
: what customers won't buy.

No, the market has decided that they won't buy desktop computers that
cost as much as their automobiles and don't perform any better than
commodity desktops.  Now that DEC has something approaching the
commodity price point with better performance don't you think they
should test the market again?

: > To me,
: > that's even more daft - when they become an NT-only vendor they loose
: > any differentiation between them and the zillion other people that can
: > provide NT boxes.

: They will not become NT-only in the markets which will buy OpenVMS or
: Digital UNIX.

: Now, if people stop buying OpenVMS and Digital UNIX for high-end servers,
: that will be a different story.

If you limit OVMS and DU to high-end servers, cost per unit sold skyrockets
and NT gets more wind added to its sails.  My original statements stand.

