Ben Myers wrote:
> 
> Joe,
> 
> Beware of "PC" PCI cards for an Alpha.  The BIOS EPROM is different,
> at minimum, even tho all other parts of card may be identical.  The
> "PC" BIOS has x86 instructions executed by an Intel x86 CPU.  An
> "Alpha" BIOS would be loaded with Alpha instructions executed by an
> Alpha CPU.  Clear enough??? Ben Myers

Huh?  I work in the engineering group that develops the Digital DE450,
DE500, DEFEA, and DEFPA EISA and PCI network adapters.  We don't design
one version for Alpha and another for PC's.  In fact, our adapters (the
same ones) work on systems regardless of the processor (Intel, Alpha,
MIPS, and PowerPC just to name a few).

There is no BIOS EPROM on the adapters, so there's no problem with the
EISA or PCI boards being installed and powered-up on an Alpha box.  Of
course, as someone already mentioned, you need to have the OS drivers
that supports the cards, but that's always the case with a new platform.

Hope this eliminates some confusion.
