In article <5aq53n$k1t@decius.ultra.net>, benmyers@ma.ultranet.com says...
>
>Joe,
>
>Beware of "PC" PCI cards for an Alpha.  The BIOS EPROM is different,
>at minimum, even tho all other parts of card may be identical.  The
>"PC" BIOS has x86 instructions executed by an Intel x86 CPU.  An
>"Alpha" BIOS would be loaded with Alpha instructions executed by an
>Alpha CPU.  Clear enough??? Ben Myers
>

The SRM console actully contain an x86 instruction to execute the x86 BIOS 
code in the video card.  This is used to display characters on the video 
monitor while at the console >>> prompt, and during the early bootstrap phase 
of OpenVMS and Unix.  After enough of the O.S. has been loaded, it switches 
over to the native O.S. display drivers.  The x86 instruction emulation in the 
SRM console allows new video cards to be supported in the SRM console without 
writing a new native driver. Of course, each O.S. must still write a native 
driver to fully support the graphics card.

