Douglas H. Borsom (borsom@netcom.com) wrote:

: Digital does not need to go after the home market. But they
: just might capture some along the way, if they can make inroads
: in the corporate desktop market.

 I pretty much agree with this, but let me be quite frank about
something: DEC has left much to be desired in trying to deal with
them.  At the company I work for, we usually deal directly with
hardware vendors instead of resellers because most see the
marketing potential of having us use their machines (and be
happy about it... a key point in working directly with the
vendor).

 DEC's philosophy, and they told us this flatly, is that they
will not work with any company directly that is not Fortune 500.
Needless to say, we have no DECs at our company, and probably
never will.  SGI and HP have worked with our company directly,
and the relationship has been beneficial for all parties.

 If DEC has any hopes of being a very successful company again,
they may want to give some thought to small and medium businesses.
Today, you cannot return to the Fortune 500 without first controlling
the Chump-Change 5 mil.  If DEC had been paying attention when
their ass was kicked in the 1980s, they would have noticed
that PCs and workstations caught on in small businesses and
education before they really started putting DEC into the
doghouse in the Fortune 500.

 I used to work in a systems group at a Big 10 university, and DECs
got thrown out the first chance we got.  The boxes and ULTRIX
worked pretty well, so why did we get rid of them?  Because DEC
didn't give us the same kind of support that Sun did.  Once
again, DEC's arrogance and lack of respect for small customers
bit them in the ass here.  All of these people who used Suns
at universities -- a large faction of the Chump-Change 5 mil
market -- have gone to work for the Fortune 500, and guess what
they're buying in droves?  Hint: think Mountain View.

 So, if DEC really wants to make some inroads into the Fortune
500 -- actually, any market what-so-ever -- with the Alpha, then
they should axe the marketing people who think "If we build it,
they will come" arrogance works on small businesses and education.
Instead, DEC needs to have a marketing and support team that are
willing to _work with_ the low-margin customers and get a solidified
base in the Chump-Change 5 mil before they can do a lot of convincing
towards large margin sales.

 - Chris


ps --

: How many of the 4 million PPros projected for 1996 do you
: think are in home computers?

 I have one at home.  I'm a geek, though.




