Be careful!

Although I'm posting this from my home account, I work for Digital
Semiconductor (the folks at Digital who build the Alpha chips), and I
have worked a LOT with the Multia/UDB (Donnie can attest to that 8-) ),
and I'd like to inject a note of reality into this performance report.

Simply put, not all Alpha chips are created equal.

The Multia is based on the 21066 aka "LCA" chip.  This was the first
cost-reduced version of the Alpha, and it did sacrifice some performance
for cost.  As a result, a 166MHz Multia will perform about the same as 
a 100MHz Pentium system (which still ain't too shabby!).  A 233MHz
Multia will perform about the same as a 133MHz Pentium.  Not a bad box
for $775 (especially considering the integrated perhipherals you get!),
but it won't blow your PPro out of the water.  Think of the 21066 as the
80486 equivalent of the Alpha lineup.

Next up the performance curve is the 21064.  This was the original
Alpha, and its performance is quite respectable.  It was used in the
DEC 2000 AXP, DECpc 150 AXP, many of the AlphaStation 200 and 400
products, the Aspen Alpine, and numerous other systems.  Think of it
as the Pentium-equivalent of the Alpha lineup.

Current state of the art is the 21164.  This is available in clock
speeds
up to 500Mhz, and I estimate that a 500MHz system will perform half
again as fast as a PPro 200 on real-world tasks.  I don't know how the
rigged benchmarks score 8-)  Think of the 21164 as the Pentium Pro of
the Alpha lineup.

If you need power, go for the 21164.  New pricing means they won't 
break the bank; you can get COMPLETE 21164-based systems from companies
like Enorex and DCG and others for $3k-$5k, depending on clock speed.

And yes, there *is* a 21264 on the horizon, but I'll stop the
comparisons
where Intel left off  8-)

