In article <32CC6A1F.562B@vulcan.achq.dnd.ca>,
Joe  <joe@vulcan.achq.dnd.ca> wrote:
>Hi there !
>
>   Can anyone tell me if there is any difference between "PC" EISA and/of 
>PCI cards and "DEC" EISA and/of PCI cards?
>
>   We want to add more functianality to our Alpha 2100.  It should be 
>less costly to procure non DEC cards if they will work.

"There shouldn't be" 

But you need to be certain your operating system (or NT) has drivers
which support these cards.  This may mean figuring out exactly what a
DEC card really is (eg, DE-435 == 21040 based PCI 10Mb ethernet card)
& purchasing an equivalent commidity pee-cee part.

For example, I've successfully used an SMC 100Mb PCI ethernet card in
an AlphaStation 500 under Digital UNIX -- it has the same 21140 chip
as the DE500, and appears to function quite well.

I'm rather curious if anybody has used commidity $59 NCR-810 based
fast PCI SCSI cards & $200 ISP-1020 based PCI SCSI cards, rather than
their expensive DEC-branded relatives which cost 2-6 times as much.

If your sable is an early one, with a limited number of PCI slots, you
might really want to run a genuine DEC card.  For example, they don't
advertise it, but the io-module that comes in an AS 600 (2 ISP-1020s &
a 21040 behind a PCI-PCI bridge) works wonderfully in an alphaserver.
Using one of these, we've managed to get 2 PCI F/W SCSI controllers &
2 PCI 100Mb/s ethernet cards into our group's demi-sable.  Very handy,
considering it has only 3 PCI slots.  Not a supported configuration,
but its been quite stable for us under Digital UNIX 3.2G.

Cheers,

Drew







