I'm not very optimistic, but I'll give you some feedback on where
I think DEC is going in all this.

I think they've cut enough employees and products to become a
much more focused company.

They've gotten away from thinking they have to be a player
in every segment (e.g. consumer PCs) and are trying to focus
on things they know they can make money doing.

Gee, even their latest TV ads are sensible!

The MIPS and PPC withdrawls show you have to have a significant
advantage over Intel to make an impact.  For whatever reason the PPC
chip just hasn't ramped up the performance (I surely can't understand
why IBM/Moto can't ramp the clock rate up instead of staying at Intel's
rates while a startup seems to be able to).  It seems no one vendor was
interested in carrying the MIPS ball forward.

My guess is DEC's strategy is to keep making better and faster servers
which is a very active market now.  This is one place that very large
address spaces (i.e. 64 bits) have a measurable advantages.  

I think they'll de-emphasize desktops more and more, and let cloners 
give the desktop Alpha market a good push while DEC makes the money
selling the chips and/or chip licenses.

I think they hope that when everyone else is sold on NT being the
server OS of choice they will be there with a bunch of fast Alpha NT
servers to sell.  IMHO NT has a bunch of ground to make up in the
server OS role relative to UNIX and OVMS, and DEC should also be
capitalizing on that, but unfortunately I don't see that happening with
the same vigor as the push towards NT.  It's clear that Alpha will be
the first chip running 64-bit NT and that DEC is porting NT to its most
powerful servers, not just the midrange ones.  This should generate
at least some market interest.  One data point is the TPC-C benchmark
results.

If you look at the TPC-C benchmark results, 64 bits is a solid win and
there is a large price advantage to using NT and SQL versus Unix and
Oracle.  Comparing one Compaq midrange to DEC Alpha midrange result,
there was a 25% or so price advantage that Compaq has due to the
these factors alone.

Also, contrast DEC to companies like Sun and SGI, which are hard-over
UNIX companies.  They have a lot of cost maintaing the OS and designing
new chips, but they don't have their own chip making talent and
facilities so they are highly dependent on others.  If those companies
decide they can do bettter things with their plants such as licensing
an Intel-clone cpu design, things get rough.  Also if these companies
stumble and can't/don't keep up in technology you have to scramble to
find a replacement.

And if the market truly does go to NT, you have go hat-in-hand to Mr.
Gates and start asking for an NT port, start training your staff to
deal with NT customers, etc.  I have no doubt Mr. Gates will be glad to
have them, and will have no concern about hurting DEC in the process,
but I think no one at DEC expects Mr. Gates to be concerned about
anyone else other than Microsoft just like Mr. Ellison cares only for
Oracle etc.

I don't know much about the Apple/Next environment i.e. what it will be
and when it will be usable.  It seems to me they have a lot of work to
do to get to the point of having an environment that I would feel good
about putting on my nephew's Apple system, and it doesn't feel to me
that they are being realistic on the time this will take, but it seems
Apple hasn't had a realistic software business plan for years.

