Based on the recent discourse on DEC and its PC strategy, I thought I'd 
add my own two cents to the debate. The following is an article that 
originally appeared in my DEC-market newsletter, Shannon Knows DEC. 
(Comments on this article, or requests for further information on SKD, 
can be directed to shannon@world.std.com.)


PCBU Update: Getting Better All the Time

( c )  1997 by Terry C. Shannon, Publisher, Shannon Knows DEC

Bloated channels. Imminent outsourcing. A desktop Dien Bien Phu. 
Marketplace abdication. Divestiture. Such were the uniformly-bleak themes 
embodied in last years news coverage of Digitals Personal Computer 
Business Unit.

SKD recently met with PCBU Vice President and General Manager Bruce 
Claflin to obtain a status report on Digital's much-maligned PC business. 
The PCBU still isn't quite where Mr. Claflin wants it to be, but things 
do appear to be getting better all the time.

Claflin has been at the helm of the PCBU for slightly over a year, during 
which time the unit has encountered--and, for the most part, successfully 
transited--its share of troubled waters. "When I came on board, the PCBU 
was regarded as a little more than an appendage to Digital," said 
Claflin. "We've gone from appendage to core business [as a WNT driver and 
volume channel]; from covering every market known to man to focusing on 
client/server computing; and from loss to near break-even."

Gone is the maniacal focus on market share that helped plunge the PCBU 
into a Fine Mess early last year. Similarly, Bruces mandate to refine the 
Alpha/Intel positioning component of the erstwhile WNT strategy has 
fortified DEC's position in the high-volume WNT-on-Intel market. The 
decision to abdicate the dog-eat-dog retail PC space has been vindicated 
by the falling fortunes of vendors such as Olivetti--and channels such as 
Best Buy Stores--in this brutally competitive arena.

Financial Times

On the financial front, things are definitely looking up. While Claflin 
refused to guarantee that the PCBU will break-even in 2FQ97, he was 
confident that the unit is closing the gap. "Asia has always been our 
best market, and it continues to do well. Europe has been in the green 
for more than a quarter, and our US business is out of the red zone and 
into the yellow," he explained. Significantly, the US has been a chronic 
problem for the PCBU, due in part to the ill-fated FY95-96 retail sales 
thrust. As for Europe, Claflin says that the PCBUs 1FQ97 European take 
ensured that DECs first quarter was bleak, not disastrous.

Outsourcing Sorcery

For reasons best known to the Fourth Estate, the PCBU's outsourcing plans 
have long been a topic of conjecture. According to Bruce, the build/buy 
decision will be revisited each time a new platform is produced. 
Currently, all mobile products are outsourced, while desktops and servers 
are designed and produced in-house. The parts commonality shared by 
desktop and servers renders internal production a no-brainer, says Claflin.

Fine-Tuning the Channels

A year ago, the PCBU's channels strategy was a big part of the problem. 
Today, its part of the solution. The PCBU is writing new contracts for 
all of its channels partners. Effective on January 1, the PCBU will deal 
with some 10 to 15 "Platinum Partners," distributors whose projected 
sales volumes and demonstrated commitment (e.g. investments) to DEC's PC 
business will garner special terms, conditions, and discounts. 
Second-tier, or Gold partners, will be extended attractive terms as well.

From a compensation standpoint, Claflin is pragmatic. Digital sales reps 
no longer are paid based on sales into the channel, sales-out is what 
counts today. Similarly, there's no distinction between Alpha and Intel 
platforms. DEC's increasing success with low-end Intel servers validates 
these revised metrics.

CPU Wars

This strategy dovetails neatly with the platform agnosticism espoused by 
Claflin. A year ago, the DEC sales force was conditioned to lead with the 
Alpha architecture when dealing with WNT customers, then fall back to 
Intel only if the customer expressed a strong preference for the 
commodity processor of choice. Given DEC's erstwhile Alpha bias, this 
strategy resulted in Digital sales reps doing pre-sales support for 
Compaq, et al. As Claflin says, Mercedes doesnt get into "engine wars" 
--find that offering a choice of gas or diesel powerplants makes for a 
better company. Positioning Alpha as an Intel product line extender 
reflects a similar philosophy.

Monkey Business

Digital's "Monkey on Your Back" ad campaign--which targets the PC-centric 
WNT marketplace--has been subjected to ridicule by employees, but the 
spokes-simians are a Big Hit among customers. Claflin cited a recent 
readership survey of two major IT publications wherein the DECchimps 
bested 50 competitive ads in brand recognition and recall. Four out of 
five readers noted the ads, associated them with DEC, and actually read 
the text accompanying the candid monkey photos. In summary, the monkey 
campaign is paying off. 

Gartner Group Resets Its Watch

Last August, market researcher Gartner Group added a PCBU product Problem 
Watch to an existing financial Problem Watch for DEC itself. According to 
Gartner, the PCBU was hamstrung by red ink, channels glut, questionable 
focus, and unresolved outsourcing issues. On December 10, Gartner took 
the PCBU off the watch list. The firm says that the PCBU is demonstrating 
notable financial and operational improvements, together with a continued 
focus on client systems and in-house product development and assembly. 
Quite a turnaround from a firm who earlier this year said there was an 80 
percent probability that DEC would exit the PC business.

The PCBU itself executed a quite a turnaround in 1996. Instead of PCBU 
Blues, near-term profitability seems to be in the cards for Claflin & Co.






