By "these", I assume you mean the switched sockets.  That's how I would
do it.

>  The
>two CPU cabs (2*BA23 boxes) I am going to plug into the 2 (presumably)
>unswitched sockets. So Qu 1.  IS this ok?.

If, indeed, what you have are two BA23 boxes, and you want to be able
to turn them on and off independent of each other, then the unswitched
sockets is the place to go.

If you want the power switch on one BA23 to control power to the other,
I'd plug the "slave" one into a switched outlet.

>On the back I have a toggle switch marked A-o-B and about 4 of the
>connectors that link the power controllers and CPUs together.  I have
>two controller connectors on the back of the CPU cabs, one on the
>master, the one with the switch and one on the second one.
>
>I want to connect the CPU system so that it switches every thing on.
>So, I assume that the master CPU is plugged into the unswitched output
>and a cable goes from the control outlet on its back to one of the
>outlets on the power controller.  Qu 2 Which one?.

You didn't specify what the exact model number of the power controller
is, but on most of the DEC ones it didn't matter which one you used.
There was at least one controlelr that had a "delayed-on" power bus connector,
but if you have one in a single power-controller system, you'd just
leave this connector unused.

>I assume that the second BA23 wants to have power applied when the
>system starts so its power cable should be plugged into the switched
>sockets.  Qu 3 is this correct?.

Yep.

>Qu 4 which setting should the toggle switch be on ? A-0-B ?

None of mine are labelled A-0-B; perhaps this was only used on
controllers sold in Europe or something.  All of mine have
positions "local on", "off", and "remote on".  For the situation
you describe, "remote on" is appropriate.  This is usually the
leftmost position of the toggle.

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



