Robin Birch <robin@falstaf.demon.co.uk> writes:

>Dear All,
>I am just setting up a system in a DEC cabinet.  It has the normal power
>control module in the base and I can't remember what goes where or how
>the switch should be set.

>On the front I have two banks of sockets, one with about 6 or 8 and one
>with 2.  I assume that the 6/8 ones are switched whilst the 2 are not.

Does the power controller have a DEC number on it (probably something like
861, 872, etc)? If so, please post it and I'll see if I've got any
definite info on it.

>As a result I am going to put the disc drives (2*RA81) into these.  The
>two CPU cabs (2*BA23 boxes) I am going to plug into the 2 (presumably)
>unswitched sockets. So Qu 1.  IS this ok?.

I _think_ that the 2 sockets on their own are unswitched, the remainder
are switched, as you suggest. These things are pretty simple inside, so if
you remove the cover you should be able to trace (at least) the mains path
from the input cable to the outlets. The 'switch' is normally (at least on
the older units I have) a large 'contactor' mounted on the chassis.

In a system with multiple CPU boxes (like your 2 BA232's) I _think_ it's
normal to plug the 'master' one into an unswitched outlet and all others
into switched outlets. Leave the switches (if any) on the slave boxes in
the 'on' position, so that they are controlled by the mains input. But see
below for more information.

>On the back I have a toggle switch marked A-o-B and about 4 of the
>connectors that link the power controllers and CPUs together.  I have
>two controller connectors on the back of the CPU cabs, one on the
>master, the one with the switch and one on the second one.

>I want to connect the CPU system so that it switches every thing on.
>So, I assume that the master CPU is plugged into the unswitched output
>and a cable goes from the control outlet on its back to one of the
>outlets on the power controller.  Qu 2 Which one?.

Right. All the control connectors on the power controller are connected in
parallel. The 3 pins are (in some order) ground, ground-for-off,
ground-for-on, with off having priority over on. The master CPU box
connects the ground-for-on pin to ground when it is turned on, either via
an extra contact on the power switch, or by circuitry in the PSU module,
which means that the PSU _must_ be powered from an unswitched outlet so
this circuit can work. I _think_ the BA23 is of this type (almost all
Unibus boxes, of which I have more experience, don't care about this).

Now, on _some_ boxes, there is an internal power controller in the PSU -
the BA11-K is of this type. On those units, you can plug _all_ of them
into unswitched outlets and link the power control cables between them.
Turning on the CPU box will activate the power controllers inside the
other PSUs, turning them on also. I don't think you can do that with a
BA23, though

>I assume that the second BA23 wants to have power applied when the
>system starts so its power cable should be plugged into the switched
>sockets.  Qu 3 is this correct?.

What I would do is the following. Connect the 'master' CPU box to an
unswitched outlet. Plug everything else (slave boxes, drives, etc) into
switched outlets. Connect a 3-pin control cable from the socket on the
back of the CPU PSU unit to any of the control connectors on the power
controller. Set the power controller to 'remote'

Turning on the CPU box will then activate the relay in the power
controller and turn on everything else.

>Qu 4 which setting should the toggle switch be on ? A-0-B ?

Hmm... Mine are all labeled 'Local-Off-Remote'.

In the position 'Off', the switched outlets are always off. The controller
is disabled.

In 'local' the switched outlets are always on, no matter what
'ground-for-on' signal does. Note that AFAIK 'ground-for-off' still turns
things off - this is used for emergency overheating shutdown, etc.

In 'remote' the controller operates as described above.

Connect the mains input of the controller to the mains. Plug some suitable
load (a table lamp?) into one of the switched outlets - UK PDP11 hackers
quickly find that a US 230V plug to a UK 13A socket lead is very useful
:-). Turn on the controller's breaker and fiddle with the toggle switch.
In one end position, you'll find the controller turns on, and the load is
energised. This is 'local'. Set the switch to the other end position
('remote'), plug in the CPU box and the controller cable, and make sure
you can control the load from the CPU power switch. If so, then connect up
the rest of the system. 

>Cheers

>Robin
>Robin Birch     robin@falstaf.demon.co.uk

>M1ASU           Old computers and radios always welcome

