In article <5aop9c$4iv$1@nntp.ucs.ubc.ca>,
Tim Shoppa <shoppa@alph02.triumf.ca> wrote:
>>I have a twin BA23 box in the middle of a three bay cabinet.  The RA81s
>>are in the top and bottom bays.
>It is more traditional to put the CPU in slot 1, and the memory in
>the next slot.

More to the point, the uVAX II won't work unless the CPU is above the
memory -- the CPU card only has PMI attachments on the bottom side of the 
CD interconnect.  While you could put the CPU in slot 2 and memory in 
slot 3, it would just be a waste of a slot -- you can't put Qbus cards 
(apart from Qbus memory) above the CPU either as the CPU will never grant 
the bus to them there..

>Keep in mind the rules regarding CD slots in a BA23.  In a regular
>"single" BA23 box the first three slots don't have any Q-bus
>signals in the CD side.  I don't know how things work in a "twin"
>box.

The twin configuration is just two stock BA23s strapped together.
(There's a bit of extra metalwork to give you one big distribution panel
instead of two piddly ones, the bottom box just has a loopback plug in
the socket where the power switch would usually go and the control panel
tends to be minus the Halt andd Restart buttons, but that's about where
the differences end.)

>Your system still sounds rather small, but check out the power
>supply harnesses, especially if you're putting a KDA50 in.  Discolored
>insulation on the wires or connectors indicates that they should
>immediately be replaced.  Once one of the 5V conductors opens up,
>the rest have to carry the load it was carrying.  Then they heat
>up even more, and open up too.  "Smoke Signals" usually begin
>coming out when there are only two or three good conductors left.

Common practice is to put the KDA50 in the second chassis, and the rest
of the system in the first box.  Better still, get the power ratings for
the boxes (beware the BA23 PSUs with only a 6A rating on the 12V rail)
and components, and do the sums yourself.

The power harness (located in the drive bay) should have white connectors 
and discrete, coloured wires.  If it hasn't, you should talk nicely to 
your local digits about replacing it -- it's a cheap part, and depending 
on who you talk to, there's a fair chance of getting it free (given that 
failures can result in fire).  And you should do this even if the power 
load is low -- the old style connectors deteriorate over time.

-- 
