>The power harness (located in the drive bay) should have white connectors 
>and discrete, coloured wires.  If it hasn't, you should talk nicely to 
>your local digits about replacing it -- it's a cheap part, and depending 
>on who you talk to, there's a fair chance of getting it free (given that 
>failures can result in fire).  And you should do this even if the power 
>load is low -- the old style connectors deteriorate over time.

Even the "new style" ones will eventually give up the magic smoke,
especially in a heavily-populated box.  I just make them myself from
connectors from Digi-Key.  I believe my design - where all the
circuits have the same length of wire - is superior than DEC's
where some conductors are longer or shorter than others.  I like
to think that the current is shared more evenly among the
paralleled conductors, though I have no way of proving this (
if I put an ammeter in series with any one of the lines, the
current in that line would drop astoundingly as a result of
the ammeter's impedance.)

The damage to the harness that eventually happens once circuits
begin opening up is impressive.  Wire insulation gets charred, the
nylon on the connectors blackens and melts, and the contact pins
lose any and all springiness.

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



