Here is some background and an update on where we are now.  

     We started with a Winows95 on a Starion 931 with a sound card,
speakers, CD, and USR sportster external modem, all of which worked.  I
connected a Thrustmaster joystick to the game port on the sound card,
turned the computer on, and started the joystick utility in the control
panel.  The joystick utility didn't detect the joystick, and gave a
message that the joystick either "wasn't connected, or there was a
problem with the connection".  I then rechecked the connection, rebooted
several times, and got the same results.  I checked the Device Manager,
and it showed one device under "Sound, video, and game controllers", a
sound card.  I looked at the properties, but I didn't record what kind
of ESS1688 card we had  and the drivers it was using.
     I then decided to check the Digital Internet site for any known
problems, and new drivers.  I found the "ad90ess4.exe" update,
downloaded it, and tried to install it.  I deleted the old device with
the Device Manager, and went through the "Add New Hardware" steps, but
drivers didn't install properly.  I ended up without sound, and the
Device Manager reporting a conflict with the ESS1688 device.  When I
checked the "Resources" tab of the ESS1688 device, in the "Conflicting
Device List" list box, it said "No Conflicts".  I then opened the
computer to confirm that it was an ESS1688 that was in the machine, (I
didn't see any label or other identifying marks on the card, so I�m
still assuming we have a 1688) and I noticed that the "plug and play"
jumper was set to "disabled".  I changed the jumper to "enabled" and
turned the computer on.  At the start of Win95 it gave a message that it
had detected a new "plug and play" device and it asked for the disk with
drivers.  I inserted the disk with the drivers I had just downloaded,
and it installed them.  I then shut down and rebooted.
     When I checked the Device Manager, there were 2 devices listed
under "Sound, video, and game controllers" one was the sound card, and
the other was a game port joystick.  The game port joystick device
however had a yellow exclamation point over it, and when I checked its
properties, it said it had a conflict.  Of course, there were no
conflicts listed in the "Conflicting Device List".  I then shut the
computer down, and turned it off.  When I turned it on and started
Win95, the sound card and the joystick worked!!!  I was able to
calibrate the joystick, and run MS "Flight Simulator for Windows 95". 
On the subsequent boot, there was no video... 
     As of now, we have been able to get the Thrustmaster working only
when the Plug and Play is enabled on the sound card.  However, when we
do get the Thrustmaster to work, on the subsequent boot, the video
doesn't work!  We know how to get to the BIOS (with key strokes and no
video) and set the "Change Configuration Data" to "No" to get the video
back. If we disable "Plug and Play" the sound card and video work, but
the joystick doesn�t.  
     The question is how do we get the Thrustmaster to work, without
having to reset the BIOS after evry reboot?

Thanks for your help.

Austin




